/* Chapter 1 — canonical topics, questions, flashcards and exam prompts. */
IFL_DATA.registerChapter({"number":1,"title":"Introduction","part":"part-i","partRoman":"I","pages":[3,20],"minutes":35,"estimates":{"f1":35,"f2":45},"difficulty":"Foundational","difficultyAlt":"foundation","learningObjectives":[{"id":"lo-ch1-1","text":"Explain the author's critique of the neoclassical/capitalist economic scenario and its outcomes.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch1-2","text":"Describe why the author regards interest-based debt as a recipe for exploitation.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch1-3","text":"Distinguish between economics as a science and an economic system, and list the three elements of a system.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch1-4","text":"Identify the four sources of problems the author names and how Islamic principles respond to them.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch1-5","text":"Summarise the milestones Islamic finance had reached and the author's caution about merely formal Shari’ah compliance.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch1-6","text":"Outline the three-part structure of the book.","origin":["f1"]},{"id":"lo-ch1-7","text":"Explain why the author sees the interest-based conventional financial system as a source of exploitation and inequitable growth","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch1-8","text":"Describe the link the author draws between excessive debt, unchecked money creation and global financial instability","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch1-9","text":"Summarize why growth alone (without distributive justice) fails to reduce poverty","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch1-10","text":"State the 'no risk, no gain' principle and why it is presented as Shari'ah's normative rule of justice","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch1-11","text":"Outline the main elements the author identifies as needed for Islamic principles to correct conventional economics","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch1-12","text":"Describe the milestones Islamic finance had reached globally by the mid-2000s","origin":["f2"]},{"id":"lo-ch1-13","text":"Explain the three-part structure of the book and what each part covers","origin":["f2"]}],"why":"Chapter 1 sets out the problem the whole book responds to: an interest-based, debt-driven financial system that the author argues produces inequality and instability. Every later rule — the prohibition of Riba, risk-sharing, asset-backed finance — is presented as an answer to the concerns raised here.","whyExpanded":"This chapter sets out the author's diagnosis of the conventional, interest-based financial system as the root cause of global inequity, unrepayable debt and financial instability, and frames Islamic finance as a risk-sharing alternative. It gives the motivation and roadmap for everything that follows in the book.","overview":"The author surveys the post-Bretton Woods economic order, argues that interest-based debt and unchecked money creation are the main culprits behind inequity, and presents Islamic economic and financial principles — risk-sharing, asset-based finance, prohibition of interest, gambling and excessive uncertainty — as a corrective. The chapter closes with the progress of Islamic finance, the conditions for it to achieve its objectives, and a map of the book.","summary":"Chapter 1 argues that the neoclassical, interest-based and debt-driven economic order has produced inequity and instability, that growth alone and welfare spending cannot correct these, and that money and finance — particularly interest — are the main culprits. Islamic principles offer checks: markets with ethics, prohibition of interest, gambling and excessive uncertainty, Zakat, returns linked to risk and value addition, and finance tied to real assets. Islamic finance had reached important milestones, but the author warns that it must be Shari’ah inspired, not merely formally compliant, to achieve its objectives.","summaryExpanded":"Chapter 1 diagnoses the conventional, interest-based global financial system as the central cause of unrepayable debt, distorted incentives and rising inequality, even amid headline economic growth. The author argues that state welfare spending cannot substitute for structural reform, and identifies interest (Riba) as the main culprit because it allows gain without risk-bearing, violating the Shari'ah principle of 'no risk, no gain'. He proposes Islamic principles — asset-backed money, trade/leasing/partnership-based contracts, government oversight of markets, and Zakat — as the needed correction, and documents Islamic finance's rapid growth into a global industry by the mid-2000s. The chapter closes by cautioning that real impact requires substantive, not merely formal, Shari'ah compliance, and outlines the book's three-part roadmap.","summarySection":null,"takeaways":["Interest-based debt is presented as a recipe for exploitation and instability.","“No risk, no gain” — return must be linked to liability for loss.","Economic system = ownership + disposal of ownership + distribution of wealth.","Four problem sources and Islamic checks: money creation, ethics-free markets, growth without distribution, passive State.","Commutative contracts may earn a return; gratuitous contracts (loans, guarantees, gifts) may not.","Financial assets must be based on real assets; time has value only through pricing of goods/usufructs.","Compliance must be Shari’ah inspired, not merely formal.","The conventional interest-based system is critiqued for generating self-perpetuating debt, wealth concentration and rising inequality (illustrated with US debt data and rising Gini coefficients).","Growth alone (without distributive justice) and government welfare spending alone cannot fix a structurally unjust system — justice must be built into production and distribution itself.","Interest (Riba) is identified as the central culprit because it allows risk-free gain; the Shari'ah counter-principle is 'no risk, no gain'.","An economic system comprises ownership, disposal of ownership, and distribution of wealth; Islamic economics differs mainly on the first and third.","Islamic economics retains markets, property and enterprise but requires active (not business-running) government oversight, asset-backed finance, and profit tied to value addition plus risk-bearing.","Contracts split into commutative (sale, leasing — profit valid) and noncommutative (gifts, guarantees, loans — gratuitous, no return permitted).","Islamic finance grew from an academic idea (pre-1970s) to a global industry (~75 countries, ~550 institutions) by the mid-2000s, gaining mainstream regulatory and academic recognition, including significant non-Muslim uptake.","The author warns that genuine impact requires substantive ('Shari'ah inspired'), not merely formal/legal, Shari'ah compliance — a theme revisited throughout the book."],"takeawayOrigins":["f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f1","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2","f2"],"checklist":["Can you explain why the author calls conventional debt a recipe for exploitation?","Can you list the three elements of an economic system?","Can you name the four sources of problems and the Islamic response?","Can you state the principle “no risk, no gain” and what it implies?","Can you distinguish commutative from noncommutative contracts with examples?","Can you outline the three parts of the book?"],"topics":[{"id":"t1.1","section":"1.1","title":"Economic Scenario in the Neoclassical Framework","pages":[3,4],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["capitalism-critique"],"intuition":"Why does a book on Islamic finance begin with a critique of the global economy? Because the author wants you to see the problem first: market forces alone have not produced balanced and equitable growth.","simpleExplanation":"After communism failed, the world relied on free markets. The author argues that markets, left alone and driven by greed and money “created out of nothing”, have widened the gap between rich and poor.","academicExplanation":["The author contrasts two failed extremes. Communism centralised control with only hypothetical ownership and collapsed within a century. Capitalism, canonised at Bretton Woods (1944), gave firms and individuals a free hand to maximise profit with minimal regard for human aspects, norms and ethics; the post-Bretton Woods order, built on excessive creation of money (particularly the US Dollar), is said to have produced “oceans” of poverty.","The resulting scenario raises concerns for mankind: behaviour guided only by self-interest; no discipline in creating high-powered money; leaving health, education and basic needs to market forces; little concern for human dignity, justice or the weak; concentration of wealth alongside hunger; and unethical practices such as deceitful advertising. Keynes is quoted to support the view that private and social interests do not automatically coincide."],"examExplanation":"Neoclassical/capitalist framework: profit maximisation with minimal ethical constraint and excessive money creation after Bretton Woods → unbalanced, inequitable growth; greed and self-interest do not automatically serve the public interest (Keynes).","keyPoints":["Market forces have failed to achieve balanced and equitable growth within and between countries.","Communism: centralised control, hypothetical ownership — collapsed in under a century.","Capitalism: free hand to maximise profit with minimal consideration of ethics.","Money created “out of nothing” strengthens exploitation and widens inequality.","Keynes: enlightened self-interest does not always operate in the public interest.","Keynes is quoted: enlightened self-interest does not automatically serve the public interest."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Gold standard and Bretton Woods (footnote)","kind":"textbook","text":"The book notes that the Bretton Woods agreement linked currencies to the US Dollar, redeemable at $35 per ounce of gold; in 1971 even this gold–currency link was abolished and gold became an ordinary commodity priced in paper currency."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"The author rejects markets and private enterprise.","right":"No — later in the chapter he states that market mechanisms, private property, self-interest and competition are integral to Islamic economics. His critique is of markets without ethical limits and of unchecked money creation."}],"relatedTopics":["t1.2","t1.8"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to the author, what characterised the post-Bretton Woods system?","options":["A strict gold standard","Excessive creation of monies, particularly the US Dollar","Centralised state ownership","Zero debt accumulation"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The author links the post-Bretton Woods system to excessive creation of money, especially the US Dollar (p. 3)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch1-t1"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch1-t1","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"1.1","sectionTitle":"Economic Scenario in the Neoclassical Framework","title":"Failures of the Neoclassical/Capitalist Economic Scenario","covers":["t1.1"],"overview":"The author opens by arguing that, since the collapse of centralized (communist) economies, market-force-driven capitalism has also failed to deliver balanced and equitable growth.","simpleExplanation":"After communism collapsed, the world leaned on free markets to fix the economy, but free markets alone did not spread prosperity fairly. A small elite gained hugely while inequality and poverty persisted almost everywhere.","academicExplanation":"Ayub traces the post-Bretton Woods (1944) capitalist order, which gave capitalist countries, firms and individuals a free hand to maximize profit with minimal regard for human/ethical considerations. Excessive creation of money (especially the US Dollar after the 1971 gold-link abolition) is presented as generating 'oceans' of poverty. Communism is described as the opposite extreme (hypothetical ownership, centralized control) that collapsed after a short cycle of under a century. Neocapitalism, by contrast, has persisted longer due to political/institutional support (e.g. effective control over the IMF and World Bank) but is characterized by unchecked 'greed', money creation out of nothing, and a widening gap between rich and poor. The author lists resulting concerns: self-interested human behaviour with no ethical constraint, undisciplined money creation, contradictory policies (leaving health/education/basic needs to market forces while claiming to fight poverty), disregard for human dignity, exploitation of the weak by the influential, and unethical marketing practices. He quotes Keynes on the non-coincidence of private and social interest to support this diagnosis.","examExplanation":"Neoclassical capitalism (post-1944 Bretton Woods) gave firms/individuals a free hand to maximize profit with little regard for ethics; unchecked money creation (especially post-1971 US Dollar) worsened global poverty. Communism collapsed due to its own extremism; neocapitalism persists via institutional/political power but shows the same core flaws: self-interest without ethical limits, undisciplined money creation, and elite exploitation of the weak, contradicting Keynes's observation that private and social interests do not automatically coincide.","keyPoints":["The 1944 Bretton Woods system tied currencies to the US Dollar (fixed to gold); the gold-currency link was abolished in 1971, after which the Dollar became the world's de facto reserve currency backing.","Communism collapsed after less than a century due to its own extremist, unbalanced approach to ownership and control.","Neocapitalism has lasted longer due to political/institutional support (e.g. influence over the IMF and World Bank) but shows the same tendency toward crisis.","The author lists specific concerns: self-interested behaviour with no ethical discipline, undisciplined money creation, contradictory social policy, disregard for human dignity, exploitation of the weak, and deceptive marketing practices.","Keynes is quoted to argue that private and social interests do not automatically coincide, undercutting the premise that unregulated self-interest serves the public good."],"definitions":[],"conditions":[],"principles":["Private and social interests do not automatically coincide (per Keynes, as cited by the author) — a market left to pure self-interest does not reliably produce socially just outcomes."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"The Bretton Woods and post-1971 Dollar system","kind":"textbook","text":"In 1944, 35 sovereign nations fixed their currencies to the US Dollar, itself redeemable at $35 per ounce of gold. In 1971 the gold-currency link was abolished, after which the Dollar (as world reserve currency) became, in the author's account, a tool through which the US could sustain deficits that other debtor nations could not."}],"commonConfusions":["Students may assume the author blames only communism or only capitalism; he critiques both extremes, though the chapter's main target is the interest/debt-driven capitalist model."],"distinctions":["Communism: hypothetical ownership, centralized control, collapsed quickly. Neocapitalism: diversified but still concentrated control over resources, sustained longer by institutional/political power."],"relatedConcepts":["Bretton Woods system","Interest-based finance","Distributive justice"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.1","pages":[3,4]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Capitalism","b":"Communism","text":"Capitalism concentrates resources indirectly through many channels, backed by strong institutional support; communism centralised control while ownership was only nominal — the author sees both as unbalanced extremes (pp. 3–4)."}]},{"id":"t1.2","section":"1.2","title":"Conventional Debt: A Recipe for Exploitation","pages":[4,6],"tier":"core","concepts":["riba","debt"],"intuition":"If yesterday’s debt can be repaid only by borrowing more today, who ultimately pays? The author uses this question to explain why he sees interest-based debt as exploitative.","simpleExplanation":"Interest-based borrowing creates debt that keeps growing. Governments pay the interest by raising taxes and spending export earnings, while the benefits flow to a few. Even rich countries like the US have become huge debtors.","academicExplanation":["The author presents the interest-based financial system as a major hurdle to distributive justice because it creates “unrepayable debt”, making one class richer and leaving others poorer. Excessive debt and its servicing are its striking features: yesterday’s debt is repaid with today’s debt.","For underdeveloped countries, interest costs are met through higher taxes and charges without a quid pro quo, and foreign-exchange earnings are consumed by debt servicing. This increases the share of risk-free capital relative to risk-based capital and business, contributing to business failures, unemployment and inequality. Debt relief packages have not solved the underlying problem, and falls in external debt have been offset by rising domestic debt.","Developed countries are also affected: the author cites the US transformation into the world’s largest debtor, sustained by the Dollar’s reserve-currency status, and rising inequality measured by the Gini coefficient. In Shari’ah, debt liability is subject to strict accountability on the Day of Judgement."],"examExplanation":"Interest-based system → unrepayable, self-perpetuating debt; debt servicing via taxes and export earnings; rising share of risk-free vs risk-based capital → unemployment, inequality, instability. Debt relief has not addressed the root cause. Shari’ah: strict accountability for debt.","keyPoints":["Interest creates “unrepayable debt” and a vicious circle of poverty.","Debt servicing crowds out socio-economic amenities in developing countries.","Risk-free capital grows relative to risk-based capital.","Interest-based credit encourages unproductive and wasteful spending, reducing savings and real investment.","Combined with inflation, the system becomes a recipe for instability.","Debt servicing is paid through taxes and charges on consumption, with no quid pro quo for citizens.","Easy credit encourages wasteful spending, lowering savings, real investment and employment."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"US debt figures cited","kind":"textbook","text":"The author cites a 2004 US deficit of $668 billion (5.7% of GDP), net external liabilities over $2.7 trillion, and US national debt exceeding $9 trillion, as evidence that even developed economies depend on debt."},{"title":"Rising inequality (Gini)","kind":"textbook","text":"US Gini coefficient cited as rising from 0.394 (1970) to 0.408 (1990) to 0.462 (2000)."},{"title":"Debt relief offset by domestic borrowing","kind":"textbook","text":"External debt of developing countries fell from about 45% of GNI (1999) to about 40% (2003), but the fall was offset by rising domestic debt at higher interest rates — the overall burden did not go away (p. 5)."},{"title":"The United States as the largest debtor","kind":"textbook","text":"Repeated current-account deficits turned the US from a major investor in the 1970s into the world’s largest debtor; only the Dollar’s reserve-currency status shields it (p. 5)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Debt relief solves the problem the author describes.","right":"The author argues debt relief packages failed to resolve poverty and that declines in external debt were offset by rising domestic debt."}],"relatedTopics":["t1.5","t3.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which shift does the author say interest-based debt produces in an economy?","options":["More risk-based capital relative to risk-free capital","More risk-free capital relative to risk-based capital","Complete elimination of debt","Equal distribution of wealth"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"He argues the system leads to an ever-increasing share of risk-free capital vis-à-vis risk-based capital (p. 5)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch1-t2"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch1-t2","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"1.2","sectionTitle":"Conventional Debt: A Recipe for Exploitation","title":"Conventional Debt as a Recipe for Exploitation","covers":["t1.2"],"overview":"The author argues that the interest-based financial system generates unrepayable, self-perpetuating debt that entrenches inequality between debtor and creditor nations/classes.","simpleExplanation":"Borrowing at interest creates a trap: today's debt often gets repaid only by taking on more debt tomorrow. This keeps poor countries and poor people stuck, while a smaller group of creditors keeps getting richer, and even rich countries like the US have become chronically indebted.","academicExplanation":"Ayub argues that excessive debt and its servicing are hallmarks of the interest-based mechanism: debt is habitually rolled over rather than repaid, crippling growth and undermining poverty-reduction efforts by institutions like the World Bank and IMF. He contends no one is accountable for who eventually pays the debt, whereas in Islamic Shari'ah, debt liability carries strict accountability on the Day of Judgement. He links underdeveloped countries' (UDCs) economic problems to debt accumulation and interest-servicing costs, which governments fund via rising taxes without corresponding public benefit. This produces an ever-growing share of risk-free capital relative to risk-based capital, business failures, unemployment and wealth concentration. He cites data: developing-country debt fell from about 45% of GNI (1999) to about 40% (2003) after partial relief to 27 (mostly Sub-Saharan African) countries, but domestic debt rose to offset this, and trade barriers by developed countries persisted. He extends the critique to the US itself, which moved from being a major international investor in the 1970s to the world's largest debtor, its immunity resting solely on the Dollar's reserve-currency status (2004 deficit $668bn/5.7% of GDP; net external liabilities over $2.7 trillion; national debt past $9 trillion). Rising Gini coefficients in the US (0.394 in 1970 to 0.462 in 2000) and Britain, and inequality even in fast-growing China, are cited as evidence that the system generates inequality broadly, not just in poor countries.","examExplanation":"Interest-based debt is self-perpetuating (rolled over rather than repaid), diverting government revenue to debt-servicing rather than public welfare, and shifting the balance from risk-based to risk-free capital, producing business failure, unemployment and wealth concentration. The author supports this with debt-relief statistics (27 countries, mostly Sub-Saharan Africa, debt-to-GNI falling from ~45% to ~40% between 1999-2003) and US data (2004 deficit $668bn = 5.7% GDP; debt over $9 trillion) plus rising Gini coefficients (US: 0.394 in 1970 to 0.462 in 2000) as evidence of growing inequality even in developed economies.","keyPoints":["Interest-based debt tends to be perpetually rolled over ('yesterday's debt repaid by today's new debt') rather than genuinely repaid.","Debt servicing consumes tax revenue and export/remittance earnings without providing offsetting public benefit, worsening the fiscal position of poor countries.","Islamic Shari'ah treats debt liability as subject to strict accountability even in the Hereafter, unlike conventional debt where accountability for repayment is diffuse.","Even the US, the world's largest economy, became the world's largest debtor nation, sustained only by the Dollar's reserve-currency status.","Rising Gini coefficients in the US and Britain, and inequality in fast-growing China, show the interest-based system generates inequality even amid strong headline growth."],"definitions":[{"term":"Gini coefficient","definition":"A measure of inequality of income distribution in an economy, cited by the author as having risen in the US from 0.394 (1970) to 0.408 (1990) to 0.462 (2000)."}],"conditions":[],"principles":["In Islamic Shari'ah, debt liability is subject to strict accountability on the Day of Judgement, unlike the diffuse accountability of conventional sovereign/corporate debt."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"US debt trajectory cited by the author","kind":"textbook","text":"2004 US current account deficit: $668 billion (5.7% of GDP); net external liabilities over $2.7 trillion (23% of US GDP); 2005 deficit rose to $805 billion; US national debt passed $9 trillion; the author cites columnist Art Buchwald calling it 'The $9-trillion heist'."}],"commonConfusions":["This is a macro/structural critique of debt-servicing dynamics, not a claim that all borrowing is inherently impossible in Islam — the book later distinguishes benevolent loans (Qard) from interest-bearing debt."],"distinctions":["Risk-free capital (interest-based lending) vs risk-based capital (equity/trade/partnership financing) — the author frames the shift toward the former as the core structural problem."],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Debt servicing","Distributive justice","Gini coefficient"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.2","pages":[4,5,6]}}]},{"id":"t1.3","section":"1.3","title":"Growth per se May not Lead to Socio-economic Justice","pages":[6,8],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["distributive-justice"],"intuition":"Can a country grow fast and still leave most people poorer? The author says yes — growth is not the same as fair distribution.","simpleExplanation":"Economic growth alone does not guarantee that the poor benefit. Benefits do not automatically “trickle down”; without fair distribution of assets, growth can deepen inequality.","academicExplanation":["Because of rising inequality, growth alone is not a reliable indicator of socio-economic development. Steady-state growth models and trickle-down theory are said to enhance inequality of asset distribution by letting the powerful grow faster. Growth under neoliberalism does not serve the welfare function when markets function without surveillance, disclosure and transparency.","Where wealth is concentrated in big business and the countryside is feudalistic, high growth will not improve income distribution; large-scale poverty even reduces demand for manufactured goods. The author calls for a redistribution of assets and income prior to stabilisation, and for replacing risk-free with risk-related capital, attracting foreign direct and portfolio investment rather than borrowed funds."],"examExplanation":"Growth ≠ development: trickle-down fails, benefits concentrate; need redistribution of assets/income and replacement of risk-free (borrowed) capital with risk-related (direct/portfolio) investment.","keyPoints":["Trickle-down does not happen by itself.","China is cited as a high-growth economy facing worsening rural poverty.","Solution proposed: risk-related capital instead of borrowed, risk-free capital."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Rising inequality in a rich economy","kind":"textbook","text":"The US Gini coefficient rose from 0.394 (1970) to 0.408 (1990) and 0.462 (2000) — a level resembling developing countries — showing growth alone does not deliver justice (p. 6)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"The author is against growth.","right":"He argues growth per se is not enough; distribution matters, and growth should come with distributive justice."}],"relatedTopics":["t1.7","t2.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"What does the author propose instead of borrowed, risk-free funds?","options":["More sovereign borrowing","Risk-related capital, including direct and portfolio investment","Printing more money","Higher interest rates"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"He says the solution lies in replacing risk-free with risk-related capital and ensuring inflows as direct and portfolio investment (p. 8)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch1-t3"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch1-t3","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"1.3","sectionTitle":"Growth per se May not Lead to Socio-economic Justice","title":"Growth Without Distributive Justice, and the Limits of State Welfare Spending","covers":["t1.3"],"overview":"The author argues that GDP growth alone, and government social-welfare spending on top of an unjust system, cannot deliver socio-economic justice unless the underlying distributional mechanism itself is reformed.","simpleExplanation":"Just growing the economy doesn't automatically help ordinary people — 'trickle-down' often doesn't trickle down, especially where wealth is concentrated in a few hands. Similarly, government welfare spending can't fix a system that is unjust at its root; you need to change the system that creates and distributes wealth, not just patch its effects afterward.","academicExplanation":"Ayub critiques steady-state growth models and 'trickle-down theory' (TDT) as demonstrably increasing asset-distribution inequality by letting already-powerful groups grow faster than before. He cites John Perkins's view that unqualified faith in growth benefiting all of humankind is erroneous, benefiting only a small portion of the population. Using China as an example, he notes that impressive double-digit growth has coincided with worsening conditions for the rural poor as the prior communist welfare guarantees (food, healthcare, education) collapsed under market transition. Where wealth/assets are concentrated in urban big business and the countryside remains feudalistic, even high sectoral growth will not improve income distribution or reduce poverty — he argues a 'revolutionary redistribution of assets and income' is needed prior to stabilization for growth to reduce inequality. He extends this to state welfare spending (1.4): heavy government spending on social security nets cannot offset the injustice built into the conventional economic system as a whole, and can itself create moral hazard; the required fix is structural — ensuring weaker groups get a fair share at the production/distribution stage itself, not only compensatory spending afterward.","examExplanation":"Trickle-down theory and steady-state growth models tend to widen, not narrow, asset-distribution inequality, since already-powerful groups grow fastest (evidenced by China's rural poverty persisting despite double-digit growth). State social-welfare spending cannot substitute for structural reform because it addresses symptoms (via selective redistribution after the fact) rather than the root cause (unjust production/distribution mechanisms), and can create moral hazard.","keyPoints":["Steady-state growth models and trickle-down theory (TDT) tend to increase inequality by letting already-powerful groups grow fastest.","China's rural poor faced worsening conditions despite double-digit GDP growth, as prior communist-era welfare guarantees (food, healthcare, education) collapsed with market transition.","Where wealth is concentrated (urban big business, feudalistic countryside), even strong sectoral growth will not improve income distribution or reduce poverty.","The author calls for 'revolutionary redistribution of assets and income' prior to stabilization if growth is to reduce distributional inequality.","Government social-welfare spending cannot correct injustice built into the system as a whole; it can create moral hazard and cannot reach the scale of need.","The real fix must occur at the production/distribution stage itself (ensuring weaker groups get their due share), not only via after-the-fact compensatory state spending."],"definitions":[{"term":"Trickle-down theory (TDT)","definition":"The theory that growth benefiting the wealthy/powerful will eventually 'trickle down' to benefit the broader population; the author argues this has been empirically disproved, citing Malaysia's failed 1957-1970 experience with it."}],"conditions":[],"principles":["Justice/fair play must be built into the production and distribution stage of an economic system, not bolted on afterward through charity or state welfare spending."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Malaysia's 1957-1970 experience with trickle-down growth","kind":"textbook","text":"The author notes (developed further in 1.7) that Malaysia's TDT-based policy during 1957-1970 failed and contributed to the 13th May 1969 race riots, prompting a shift to a justice-with-fairness-based New Economic Policy."}],"commonConfusions":["Students may conflate 'growth is bad' with the author's actual claim: growth without a just distributional mechanism fails to achieve socio-economic justice."],"distinctions":["Compensatory welfare spending (treating symptoms after unjust distribution has occurred) vs structural justice (fair access to production and distribution itself) — the author argues only the latter is adequate."],"relatedConcepts":["Distributive justice","Trickle-down theory","Moral hazard","Socio-economic justice"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.3","pages":[6,7,8]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Growth","b":"Socio-economic development","text":"Growth measures output; development requires that its benefits reach the masses. Trickle-down growth can widen asset inequality, so the author calls for redistribution of assets and income before stabilisation (p. 7)."}]},{"id":"t1.4","section":"1.4","title":"Social Welfare Activities of the States","pages":[8,8],"tier":"detailed","concepts":["distributive-justice"],"intuition":"If governments spend so much on safety nets, why does poverty persist? The author’s answer: welfare spending cannot fix a system whose operative tools conflict with its goals.","simpleExplanation":"State welfare spending helps, but it cannot correct injustice built into the system itself. Weaker groups need their fair share at the stage of production and distribution.","academicExplanation":["Governments spend heavily on social security nets, yet this does not mitigate the ill effects of conventional economic and financial tools. Selective spending cannot correct system-wide imbalances and may create moral hazard. The author identifies a conflict between the operative tools of conventional economics and its normative goals: interest-based creation and allocation of funds and market-based monetary policy are described as anti-poor."],"examExplanation":"Welfare spending cannot offset system-level injustice; conflict between operative tools (interest, market-based monetary policy) and normative goals; weaker groups need a fair share at production and distribution stages.","keyPoints":["Safety nets alone cannot cater to millions of poor.","Change is needed at the level of the system, not just spending.","Charity on any scale cannot solve the problem (footnote on “Billanthropy”).","Conflict between the operative tools of conventional economics and its normative goals explains why even rich states miss their welfare aims."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Safety net vs system fix","kind":"practice","text":"A state spends heavily on cash transfers to poor households, while interest-based credit keeps flowing mainly to large borrowers with collateral. The author’s point is that transfers treat symptoms: unless the way funds are created and allocated changes, inequality is regenerated each year (p. 8)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Bigger social-security spending can by itself correct the injustice created by the financial system.","right":"The author argues selective state spending cannot offset system-wide imbalances and may create moral hazard; the system itself must change so weaker groups get their share at the stage of production and distribution (p. 8)."}],"relatedTopics":["t1.3","t1.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"True or false: the author argues that increased government welfare spending is sufficient to correct the imbalances of the conventional system.","options":["True","False"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"False. He argues imbalances created by the system cannot be corrected only by selective government spending (p. 8)."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[]},{"id":"t1.5","section":"1.5","title":"The Main Culprit","pages":[8,9],"tier":"core","concepts":["riba","risk-sharing"],"intuition":"Among all causes of economic injustice, which one does the author single out? Money and finance — specifically the institution of interest.","simpleExplanation":"The author names two main factors: poor economic management that ignores exploitation, and the way money and finance work. Interest, he says, creates “parasites” in society. The Shari’ah principle “no risk, no gain” means anyone who wants a return must also be liable for a loss.","academicExplanation":["Two main factors explain the failure of the global economic system: an inefficient modus operandi of economic management with little concern for poverty or exploitation, and the functioning of money, finance and financial markets, which play the most strategic role in creating, distributing and transferring wealth.","The institution of interest, on which governments and corporations borrow, is said to widen the rich–poor gap. The prohibition of interest in all revealed religions implies that there can be no gain without risk-sharing. “No risk, no gain” is described as the basic juristic principle of Shari’ah and a normative rule of justice; liability for loss makes investors more careful, removes the moral hazard of risk-free gains and injects discipline into the financial system."],"examExplanation":"Main culprit = interest-based money and finance (plus weak economic management). Shari’ah principle: “no risk, no gain” — return requires liability for loss; removes moral hazard of risk-free gains and adds discipline.","keyPoints":["Money is the major tool in the hands of governments.","Interest creates idle, parasitic gain (Yusuf Ali’s commentary on 2:275 is quoted).","“No risk, no gain” — the basic juristic principle.","Risk-bearing reduces moral hazard and increases discipline."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"“No risk, no gain” in two contracts","kind":"practice","text":"A lender gives 1,000,000 and is owed 1,100,000 whatever happens to the borrower’s business. A Musharakah partner gives the same 1,000,000 but shares the actual profit or loss. Only the second satisfies the principle that a return must be matched by liability for loss (pp. 8–9)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"“No risk, no gain” means Islamic finance maximises risk.","right":"It means return must be linked to bearing risk/liability; later chapters show risk can be mitigated but not eliminated."}],"relatedTopics":["t4.2","t1.9"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which principle does the author describe as the basic juristic principle of Shari’ah in this section?","options":["Caveat emptor","No risk, no gain","Time value of money","Too big to fail"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"“No risk, no gain” is described as the basic juristic principle of Shari’ah and a normative rule of justice (p. 9)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch1-t4"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch1-t4","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"1.5","sectionTitle":"The Main Culprit","title":"Interest (Riba) as the Main Culprit and the 'No Risk, No Gain' Principle","covers":["t1.5"],"overview":"The author identifies the institution of interest — and the broader functioning of money and finance — as the principal factor behind global economic injustice, and introduces the Shari'ah principle that gain must be matched by risk-bearing.","simpleExplanation":"Among all the causes of economic injustice, the biggest one is interest: lending money at a guaranteed return, no matter what happens to the borrower, creates a class of people who profit without any real risk while others bear all the risk. Islam's answer is simple: if you want a share of the gain, you must also share the risk of loss.","academicExplanation":"Ayub narrows the 'long list' of causes for global economic failure to two main factors: (1) an inefficient economic-management model with no real concern for poverty/exploitation, and (2) the functioning of money, finance and financial markets, which play the most strategic role in creating, distributing and transferring resources and wealth. He argues the institution of interest — by which governments and public/private corporations borrow — creates 'parasites' in society and widens the rich-poor gap, quoting Yusuf Ali's translation/commentary on Qur'an 2:275 contrasting legitimate trade (which increases prosperity) with usury (which encourages 'idlers' and 'cruel bloodsuckers'). He states that the prohibition of interest across all revealed religions implies a core juristic principle: 'no risk, no gain' — there can be no legitimate gain without risk-sharing; whoever wants a return must also bear liability for loss. He argues this liability motivates more careful investment decisions and removes the moral hazard of risk-free gains, injecting greater discipline into the financial system.","examExplanation":"The author identifies interest (Riba) as the central culprit behind global economic injustice, because it allows gain without risk-bearing, creating a rentier class ('parasites') while others bear all risk. He grounds this in the Shari'ah maxim 'no risk, no gain': whoever claims a return must also bear the corresponding liability for loss. This is presented as both a moral/juristic principle and a mechanism for financial discipline (it removes the moral hazard of risk-free gains).","keyPoints":["The author narrows the causes of global economic failure to two: inefficient economic management with no concern for poverty, and the functioning of money/finance/financial markets.","The institution of interest is singled out as creating a 'parasite' class and widening the rich-poor gap.","Yusuf Ali's commentary on Qur'an 2:275 is cited to contrast legitimate trade (which builds prosperity) with usury/interest (which the translation associates with exploitation).","'No risk, no gain' is presented as the basic juristic principle of Shari'ah and a normative rule of justice: return must be matched by liability for loss.","Bearing liability for loss is argued to make investors more careful and to remove the moral hazard associated with risk-free financial gains."],"definitions":[{"term":"Riba","definition":"Literally an excess or increase; technically, an increase over the principal in a loan/debt transaction or in an exchange transaction, accruing to the lender/creditor or a party to the exchange without an equivalent counter-value ('iwad) given in return."}],"conditions":[],"principles":["'No risk, no gain' — the basic juristic principle of Shari'ah and a normative rule of justice: entitlement to a return must be matched by liability for a possible loss."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["The 'no risk, no gain' principle is a general Shari'ah maxim introduced here; the detailed juristic definition and categories of Riba (Riba al-Nasiah, Riba al-Fadl) are developed fully in Chapter 3, not here."],"distinctions":["Risk-free gain (interest, criticized here) vs risk-related/risk-sharing gain (the alternative the author advocates, elaborated as the basis of Islamic finance in later chapters)."],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Risk-sharing","Moral hazard","Qur'an 2:275"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.5","pages":[8,9]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Risk-free capital (interest)","b":"Risk-related capital","text":"Interest gives a return without liability for loss; under “no risk, no gain” a return is legitimate only if the provider also bears possible loss, which makes investors more careful and removes moral hazard (p. 9)."}],"principles":["No risk, no gain: entitlement to return requires bearing liability for possible loss."]},{"id":"t1.6","section":"1.6","title":"The Need of the Hour","pages":[9,10],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["riba"],"intuition":"What kind of reform does the author call for? A system built on socio-economic justice — with interest replaced by risk-related capital.","simpleExplanation":"Many Muslim scholars believe crises stem from ignoring the prohibition of interest. The author urges economists to build a system based on justice and fair play, replacing interest with risk-related investment.","academicExplanation":["Sensible people have long called for comprehensive reform of the financial system to prevent or reduce crises. A vast majority of Muslim jurists and scholars see the ultimate cause in disregarding the prohibition of interest. Justice/fair play is the raison d’être of any sustainable system and, in the Islamic worldview, cannot be given up for any other consideration.","Replacing interest with a risk-related capital and investment mechanism could help solve socio-economic ills and would inject a moral dimension, greater equity and market discipline, making the financial system healthier and more stable."],"examExplanation":"Need: a balanced, equitable order based on justice; interest is the major element creating injustice; replacing it with risk-related capital adds morality, equity, discipline and stability.","keyPoints":["Justice is the raison d’être of any sustainable economic system.","Interest is identified as the major element creating injustice.","Benefits of prohibition: moral dimension, equity, market discipline, stability.","Justice/fair play is the raison d’être of any sustainable economic system and cannot be traded off in the Islamic worldview.","Benefits of prohibiting interest: a moral dimension, greater equity and market discipline, and a healthier, more stable system."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Replacing interest with risk-related capital","kind":"practice","text":"A firm funding a factory with an interest-bearing loan must pay whatever the factory earns. Funded by Musharakah, the financier’s return rises and falls with output, which the author argues adds equity and market discipline to the system (pp. 9–10)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Islamic economists only need to design new financial products.","right":"The author asks them to build a whole model of balanced, equitable growth, with justice as the non-negotiable foundation (p. 9)."}],"relatedTopics":["t1.5","t4.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"What does the author say justice/fair play is for an economic system?","options":["An optional add-on","Its raison d’être","A cost to be minimised","Relevant only to charities"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"He calls justice/fair play the raison d’être of any sustainable economic system (p. 9)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch1-t5"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch1-t5","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"1.6","sectionTitle":"The Need of the Hour","title":"The Need of the Hour: Comprehensive Financial Reform Based on Risk-Sharing","covers":["t1.6"],"overview":"The author calls for comprehensive reform of the global financial system, arguing that disregarding the prohibition of interest is the ultimate cause of recurring financial crises.","simpleExplanation":"The world needs a serious overhaul of its financial system to prevent recurring crises. Many Muslim scholars believe the root cause of these crises is that societies ignore the prohibition on interest — a teaching common to major world religions — and that replacing interest with risk-sharing capital could fix many of these problems.","academicExplanation":"Ayub states that while there are many opinions about the ultimate cause of financial crises, a majority of Muslim jurists and scholars believe the cause lies in disregarding the prohibition of interest, a teaching shared across major religions. He argues global inequality (both inter- and intra-national) requires economists and policymakers to build a system grounded in socio-economic justice and fair play, framing this as a mission with the stakes of humanity's peace, happiness, welfare and prosperity. He specifically calls on Islamic economics scholars to pursue this with dedication, treating justice/fair play as the non-negotiable raison d'être of any sustainable economic system in the Islamic worldview. He reiterates that replacing interest with a risk-related capital and investment mechanism could resolve many socio-economic ills, additionally injecting a moral dimension and greater market discipline into the financial system.","examExplanation":"The author argues comprehensive financial-system reform is needed to prevent/minimize crises, and that most Muslim jurists/scholars trace the ultimate cause of crises to disregard for the prohibition of interest (shared across major religions). The proposed remedy is replacing interest-based risk-free capital with a risk-related capital and investment mechanism, which would also inject moral discipline into the system.","keyPoints":["A majority of Muslim jurists/scholars attribute the ultimate cause of financial crises to disregard for the prohibition of interest.","The prohibition of interest is framed as a teaching common to all major (revealed) religions, not unique to Islam.","Justice/fair play is presented as the non-negotiable raison d'être of a sustainable economic system in the Islamic worldview.","Replacing interest-based capital with risk-related capital and investment is proposed as the core reform, expected to add moral dimension and market discipline to the financial system."],"definitions":[],"conditions":[],"principles":["Justice/fair play is the raison d'être of any economic system that is to be sustainable in the long run, and cannot be compromised for any other consideration in the Islamic worldview."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":[],"distinctions":[],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Risk-sharing","Financial crisis","Socio-economic justice"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.6","pages":[9,10]}}]},{"id":"t1.7","section":"1.7","title":"Economics and Religion","pages":[10,11],"tier":"core","concepts":["islamic-economics"],"intuition":"Is Islam a drag on growth? The author rejects this and draws a key distinction between economics as a science and an economic system.","simpleExplanation":"Economic science studies how wealth is created. An economic system is based on an ideology and decides how wealth is owned, used and distributed. The Islamic system differs mainly in ownership and distribution, with the State ensuring no injustice.","academicExplanation":["The author distinguishes economics as a science (dealing with creation of wealth) from an economic system (a thought based on an ideology, relating to management of wealth distribution). An economic system comprises three elements: (1) ownership of property, commodities and wealth; (2) disposal of ownership; and (3) distribution of wealth among the people.","From this perspective the Islamic economic system differs from others only to the extent of ownership and distribution of resources among the factors of production and groups in society, with a defined role for the State to ensure no injustice. Islamic economics can balance social and economic aspects, self and social interests, and the individual, family, society and State.","Historical evidence cited: Malaysia’s shift from trickle-down theory (1957–1970), which the author links to the 1969 riots, to the New Economic Policy of growth with distributive justice; and the Muslim civilisation’s contribution to knowledge (including economic thought) from the seventh to around the eleventh century, followed by intellectual regression."],"examExplanation":"Economics as science (wealth creation) vs economic system (ideology-based; wealth distribution). Three elements of a system: ownership; disposal of ownership; distribution. Islamic system differs in ownership and distribution, with State ensuring justice.","keyPoints":["Marcus Noland is cited: nothing inherent in Islamic societies causes poor performance.","Three elements: ownership, disposal of ownership, distribution.","Property (Māl) is anything that can be possessed; wealth = property plus effort (footnote, citing Nabhani).","Malaysia’s New Economic Policy cited as growth with distributive justice."],"definitions":[{"term":"Economic system","definition":"A thought based upon an ideology, relating to management of wealth distribution in a society; comprises ownership, disposal of ownership and distribution of wealth."},{"term":"Māl (property)","definition":"Anything that can be possessed — money, commodities, immovable property, etc. (footnote on p. 10)."}],"examples":[{"title":"Malaysia’s New Economic Policy","kind":"textbook","text":"The book presents Malaysia’s move from trickle-down growth to a policy of eradicating poverty and restructuring society for justice with fairness as a macro-level example of distributive justice contributing to development."},{"title":"Malaysia’s New Economic Policy","kind":"textbook","text":"Trickle-down growth (1957–1970) ended in the 13 May 1969 riots; the New Economic Policy then pursued growth with distributive justice — eradicating poverty and restructuring society — and is credited with the country’s later success (p. 11)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"The Islamic economic system differs from other systems in every respect.","right":"The author says it differs only to the extent of ownership and distribution of resources, with a defined State role against injustice."}],"relatedTopics":["t2.4","t2.5"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which is NOT one of the three elements of an economic system listed by the author?","options":["Ownership of property","Disposal of ownership","Distribution of wealth","Fixing interest rates"],"correctAnswer":3,"explanation":"The three elements are ownership, disposal of ownership and distribution of wealth (p. 10)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch1-t6"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch1-t6","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"1.7","sectionTitle":"Economics and Religion","title":"Economics and Religion: The Elements of an Economic System","covers":["t1.7"],"overview":"The author addresses whether religion belongs in economics, distinguishes economics-as-science from an economic system, and defines the three elements that make up any economic system.","simpleExplanation":"Some people think religion has nothing useful to say about economics, or even that Islam holds Muslims back economically. The author disagrees, and points out that an 'economic system' (rules about who owns what, how they use it, and how wealth is shared) is a different thing from 'economics' as a science of creating wealth — Islam mainly shapes the former.","academicExplanation":"Ayub cites economist Marcus Noland (Institute for International Economics) rejecting the view that Islam causes Muslim poverty, arguing instead that Islam, if anything, promotes growth. He distinguishes economics as a science (dealing with wealth creation) from an economic system (a thought/ideology-based framework for wealth distribution, enabling or restricting groups' use of the means of production). He defines an economic system as comprising three elements: (1) ownership of property, commodities and wealth; (2) disposal of ownership; (3) distribution of wealth among people. He notes Islamic economics differs from other systems mainly regarding ownership and distribution of resources, with a defined State role to prevent injustice. He then gives a brief macro-historical illustration: Malaysia's failed 1957-1970 trickle-down policy led to the 13th May 1969 race riots; the subsequent New Economic Policy applying justice-with-fairness (even without full Shari'ah application) is credited with Malaysia's subsequent economic success. He also sketches the Islamic civilization's historical economic contribution (7th-11th century Muslim golden age of trade, scholarship, and economic-principle development) and its later intellectual decline, culminating in the fall of the Ottoman Empire and European colonization.","examExplanation":"The author distinguishes economics as a science (wealth creation) from an economic system (ownership, disposal of ownership, and distribution of wealth — an ideology-based framework). He rejects the claim that Islam causes economic underperformance (citing Marcus Noland) and uses Malaysia's shift from trickle-down policy (which failed, contributing to 1969 riots) to a justice-with-fairness New Economic Policy as a real-world illustration of distributive-justice principles (aligned with, though not identical to, full Shari'ah application) producing better outcomes.","keyPoints":["Marcus Noland (cited by the author) rejects the view that Islamic belief causes poor economic performance in Muslim societies; he argues Islam, if anything, promotes growth.","Economics (a science of wealth creation) is distinct from an economic system (an ideology-based framework governing ownership, disposal, and distribution of wealth).","Any economic system comprises three elements: ownership of property/wealth, disposal of ownership, and distribution of wealth among people.","Islamic economics differs from other systems mainly in its rules on ownership and distribution, with a defined State role to prevent injustice to individuals, parties or groups.","Malaysia's 1957-1970 trickle-down policy failed and contributed to the 13th May 1969 race riots; the subsequent New Economic Policy (justice with fairness, though not full Shari'ah) is credited with Malaysia's later economic success.","The author sketches a historical arc: Muslim civilization's economic/scholarly golden age (7th-11th century) followed by intellectual decline, missing the Industrial Revolution, and the eventual fall of the Ottoman Empire to European colonization."],"definitions":[{"term":"Economic system (per the author)","definition":"A thought/ideology-based framework relating to management of wealth distribution in a society, comprising ownership of property/commodities/wealth, disposal of ownership, and distribution of wealth among people."},{"term":"Mal (Property)","definition":"Anything that can be possessed, including money (e.g. gold and silver), commodities (e.g. clothes and foodstuffs), and immovable property (e.g. houses and factories); wealth is Mal together with human effort (per Nabhani, as cited by the author)."}],"conditions":[],"principles":["Economics as a science (wealth creation) is conceptually distinct from an economic system (an ideological framework for wealth ownership, use and distribution)."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Malaysia: from Trickle-Down Theory to the New Economic Policy","kind":"textbook","text":"Malaysia's 1957-1970 trickle-down-based policy failed, contributing to the 13th May 1969 race riots. The government then adopted the New Economic Policy, applying the core Islamic value of justice with fairness (though not the full Shari'ah), aiming to eradicate poverty and restructure society — credited by the author with the country's later economic achievement."}],"commonConfusions":["Malaysia's New Economic Policy is described as applying a core Islamic value (justice with fairness), not as a fully Shari'ah-compliant Islamic economic system — students should not overstate this example."],"distinctions":["Economics as a science (wealth creation, value-neutral) vs an economic system (ideology-based rules on ownership, disposal and distribution of wealth)."],"relatedConcepts":["Distributive justice","Ownership","Islamic economic system"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.7","pages":[10,11]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Economics as a science","b":"Economic system","text":"Science deals with creation of wealth; a system is ideology-based and manages ownership, disposal and distribution of wealth."}]},{"id":"t1.8","section":"1.8","title":"Islamic Principles Can Make the Difference","pages":[11,13],"tier":"core","concepts":["islamic-economics","riba","gharar","maisir","zakat","commutative-contracts"],"intuition":"If the problems come from unchecked money creation, markets without ethics, growth without distribution and a passive State, what does Islam offer? Checks on each of these.","simpleExplanation":"Islam keeps markets, private property and competition, but adds ethics and justice. It prohibits interest, excessive uncertainty and gambling; promotes Zakat; and says capital can earn a return only if it also bears the risk of loss.","academicExplanation":["The author identifies four sources of problems: (1) unchecked creation of money; (2) reliance on market forces without ethical limits; (3) emphasis on growth and profit per se without regard to distribution; and (4) the negative role of the State and regulators in allowing greed and unchecked profit. Islamic principles provide checks for all of these, focusing on clarity, lack of ambiguity, just treatment and care for others’ rights.","Market mechanisms, private property, enterprise, self-interest and competition are integral to Islamic economics. Goods are consumed or used through two main contracts — sale/purchase (trading) and leasing — supported by other contracts and rules. The fundamental feature is socio-economic and distributive justice with a comprehensive ethical system; destroying output to keep prices high is not allowed.","The system prohibits commercial interest, excessive uncertainty, gambling and games of chance, and emphasises social welfare through mutual help and Zakat (generally 2.5% of net wealth, or 5 or 10% on agricultural produce above a minimum). Capital may earn a just return provided it bears the liability or risk of loss; entitlement to profit comes from value addition and bearing business risk.","All gains in exchange transactions must be accompanied by a consideration agreed with free will. Contracts are commutative (Uqood-e-Mu‘awadha, e.g. sale and leasing — profit valid) or noncommutative (Uqood Ghair Mu‘awadha, e.g. gifts, guarantees, loans — no return). A loan cannot carry any excess over the principal, and liability for it remains until repaid or waived."],"examExplanation":"Four problems: unchecked money creation; markets without ethics; growth without distribution; passive State. Islamic response: markets + ethics; prohibit interest, gharar, gambling; Zakat; return only with risk/value addition; commutative (profit allowed) vs noncommutative/gratuitous contracts (no return — loans, guarantees, gifts).","keyPoints":["Two main contracts for goods: trading (sale/purchase) and leasing.","Right of capital to a just return only if it bears the risk of loss.","Zakat: generally 2.5% of net wealth; 5 or 10% on agricultural produce above a minimum.","Guaranteeing a financial obligation for a price involves Riba; only out-of-pocket expenses may be recovered (footnote, citing the Islamic Fiqh Academy).","A loan must be repaid until the creditor relaxes it or the debtor is declared insolvent; accountability in the Hereafter remains."],"definitions":[{"term":"Zakat","definition":"Religious obligation on Muslims with wealth above consumption needs, at a nonprogressive rate (generally 2.5% of net wealth, or 5 or 10% on agricultural produce above a minimum), distributed among the needy per verse 9:60."},{"term":"Uqood-e-Mu‘awadha","definition":"Commutative contracts (e.g. sale, leasing) in which profit or return is valid."},{"term":"Uqood Ghair Mu‘awadha","definition":"Noncommutative/gratuitous contracts (e.g. gifts, guarantees, loans) for which no return can be taken."}],"examples":[{"title":"Zakat rates","kind":"textbook","text":"Zakat is generally 2.5 % of net wealth, and 5 % or 10 % on agricultural produce above a minimum (pp. 11–13)."},{"title":"Guarantee for a price","kind":"textbook","text":"A bank that charges a fee for guaranteeing someone’s financial obligation would be taking Riba; the Islamic Fiqh Academy allows only recovery of out-of-pocket expenses (pp. 11–13, footnote)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Islamic finance rejects market mechanisms and self-interest.","right":"They are integral parts of Islamic economics, subject to ethics and State oversight."},{"wrong":"A guarantor can charge a fee for guaranteeing a loan.","right":"Guaranteeing a financial obligation for a price involves Riba; only out-of-pocket expenses may be recovered."}],"relatedTopics":["t5.8","t3.2","t4.2"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Under the principles summarised here, which transaction can legitimately carry a return?","options":["A loan","A guarantee","A gift","A sale or lease"],"correctAnswer":3,"explanation":"Return is valid in commutative contracts such as sale and leasing; loans, guarantees and gifts are gratuitous (pp. 12–13)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch1-t7"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch1-t7","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"1.8","sectionTitle":"Islamic Principles Can Make the Difference","title":"Islamic Principles Can Make the Difference: Trade, Risk-Bearing and the Basis of Entitlement to Profit","covers":["t1.8"],"overview":"The author lists the four root problems of conventional economics and explains how Islamic principles of trading, leasing and partnership, combined with Zakat, address them by tying profit entitlement to value addition and risk-bearing.","simpleExplanation":"The core problems are: money created without limit, markets with no ethical boundaries, growth pursued without caring who benefits, and governments that let greed run unchecked. Islamic economics answers this by insisting that trade must be fair and transparent, that markets work under government oversight (not free-for-all), that profit must come from adding real value and bearing real risk, and that the well-off must support the poor through Zakat.","academicExplanation":"The author lists four root problems: (1) unchecked creation of money; (2) reliance on market forces without ethical limits; (3) emphasis on growth/profit without regard to distribution; (4) a negative/passive role of the State and regulators in allowing unchecked greed. Islamic economics is presented as retaining market mechanisms, private property, enterprise, self-interest and competition (as in free markets), but channeling exchange through two principal contracts — sale/purchase (trading) and leasing — supplemented by detailed subcontracts. Its defining feature is socio-economic and distributive justice plus a comprehensive ethical system: it prohibits artificial output destruction to manipulate prices, requires government oversight so markets aren't distorted by powerful players, and grants individuals ownership/enterprise rights with profit tied to genuine value addition and risk-sharing. Structurally, it prohibits commercial interest, excessive uncertainty (Gharar) and games of chance (Maisir/Qimar), and mandates Zakat (2.5% of net wealth annually above a minimum threshold — Nisab — or 5-10% on qualifying agricultural produce, per Qur'an 9:60) for the have-nots. The governing principle for exchange is that all gains/receipts must be matched by a consideration given with free will and mutual consent; contracts are classified as commutative (Uqood-e-Mu'awadha, e.g. sale, leasing — where profit is valid) versus noncommutative (Uqood Ghair Mu'awadha, e.g. gifts, guarantees, loans — which are gratuitous/benevolent acts carrying no permissible return). A loan (Qard) must eventually be repaid unless the creditor relaxes it or the debtor is declared insolvent; accountability for debt persists in the Hereafter until the creditor waives it.","examExplanation":"Islamic economics addresses the four root problems (unchecked money creation, unbounded market forces, growth without distribution, and passive/complicit regulators) through: government-overseen markets, trade/leasing as the primary contracts of exchange, entitlement to profit tied to value addition plus risk-bearing, prohibition of Riba/Gharar/Maisir, and Zakat as a redistributive mechanism. A key structural distinction: commutative contracts (sale, leasing — Uqood-e-Mu'awadha) permit profit; noncommutative contracts (gifts, guarantees, loans — Uqood Ghair Mu'awadha) are gratuitous acts that permit no return.","keyPoints":["Four root problems identified: unchecked money creation, unbounded market forces, growth/profit pursued without distributive concern, and a passive/complicit State role.","Islamic economics retains market mechanisms, private property, enterprise and competition, but channels them through ethically bounded trading and leasing contracts.","Government has an active oversight role to prevent market distortion by powerful players — markets are not a total free-for-all.","Profit entitlement is tied to value addition and bearing business risk, differing by transaction type (trading, leasing or Shirkah/partnership).","The system prohibits commercial interest, Gharar (excessive uncertainty) and Maisir/Qimar (games of chance), and mandates Zakat for the have-nots.","Contracts are split into commutative (Uqood-e-Mu'awadha — e.g. sale, leasing; profit is valid) and noncommutative (Uqood Ghair Mu'awadha — e.g. gifts, guarantees, loans; no return is permitted since these are gratuitous/benevolent acts).","A loan (Qard) must be repaid unless the creditor relaxes it or the debtor is declared insolvent; debt accountability persists in the Hereafter until waived by the creditor."],"definitions":[{"term":"Zakat","definition":"The religious obligation on every Muslim whose wealth exceeds consumption needs, at a non-progressive rate (generally 2.5% of net wealth, or 5-10% on qualifying agricultural produce above a minimum limit), distributed among the have-nots and needy per Qur'an 9:60."},{"term":"Commutative contract (Uqood-e-Mu'awadha)","definition":"A contract (e.g. sale, leasing) in which profit or return is valid, since each party gives a consideration in exchange."},{"term":"Noncommutative contract (Uqood Ghair Mu'awadha)","definition":"A gratuitous/benevolent contract (e.g. gifts, guarantees, loans) in which no return/profit may be taken by the party providing the benefit."}],"conditions":["All gains and receipts in exchange transactions must be accompanied by a consideration stipulated with the free will and mutual consent of the parties.","A guarantor may recover only out-of-pocket expenses for providing a guarantee, not a fee/opportunity cost for the guarantee itself (charging for a guarantee involves Riba)."],"principles":["Entitlement to profit or return arises from value addition and bearing business risk, not merely from the passage of time or provision of capital alone."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["Zakat is a redistributive religious levy, not a tax on income — it is assessed on wealth/assets held above the Nisab threshold on a Zakat-calculation date, not on earnings.","'Guarantee' (Kafalah) in Islamic finance cannot be sold for a fee the way conventional loan guarantees are, since it is classified as a noncommutative, benevolent contract."],"distinctions":["Commutative contracts (sale, leasing) — profit is valid, vs noncommutative contracts (gifts, guarantees, loans) — gratuitous, no return permitted."],"relatedConcepts":["Riba","Gharar","Maisir/Qimar","Zakat","Qard (loan)","Shirkah"],"examRelevance":"core","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[11,12,13]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Commutative contracts","b":"Noncommutative contracts","text":"Return is valid in sale and leasing; no return can be taken on gifts, guarantees and loans, which are gratuitous and benevolent acts."}],"principles":["All gains in exchange must be accompanied by a consideration agreed with free will and mutual consent.","Entitlement to profit comes from value addition and bearing business risk."]},{"id":"t1.9","section":"1.9","title":"Regulating Trade and Business","pages":[13,14],"tier":"core","concepts":["asset-backing","risk-sharing"],"intuition":"If Islam supports free trade, why regulate it? Because unregulated business can harm the poor — and finance must stay linked to real assets.","simpleExplanation":"Islam supports markets and trade, but the government must ensure fair functioning. In finance, every financial asset should be backed by real assets and genuine business; time has value only through the pricing of goods and their usufructs.","academicExplanation":["Islam recognises markets and individual freedom in business, but also the possible adverse impact of unregulated business on the poor. Economists should focus on two areas: (1) the role of government — an active role not in running businesses but in ensuring smooth, accountable and transparent functioning of markets; and (2) money, banking and finance.","Islamic finance requires that all financial transactions and instruments be represented by genuine assets and business transactions. Two principles provide criteria for money creation: all financial assets must be based on real assets (not necessarily gold or silver), and the time factor has value only through the pricing of goods and their usufructs. Creating financial assets out of nothing and putting others at undefined risk is described as tantamount to cheating.","Allocation of funds on the basis of interest should be replaced by risk-related placement under partnership and genuine trading and leasing, where fund owners share risk and return with fund users. Risk can be mitigated but not totally eliminated if profit is to be legitimate."],"examExplanation":"Two focus areas: active facilitative role of government; money/banking/finance. Financial assets must be based on real assets; time value only via pricing of goods/usufructs; replace interest-based allocation with partnership, trading and leasing; risk mitigated but not eliminated.","keyPoints":["Government role: ensuring market functioning with accountability and transparency, not running businesses.","Financial assets must be based on real assets and business activities.","Time has value only through the pricing of goods and usufructs.","Risk can be mitigated but not totally eliminated if profit is to be legitimised."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Two criteria for money creation","kind":"textbook","text":"Instead of returning to gold, the author proposes that (1) all financial assets be based on real assets and (2) time be given value only through the pricing of goods and usufructs — not through lending money (pp. 13–14)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"Asset-backing means returning to the gold standard.","right":"The author says the gold standard cannot be adopted again; assets need not be gold or silver, but financial assets must be based on real assets."}],"relatedTopics":["t4.2","t4.6","t4.7"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"According to the author, in Islamic finance the time factor has value only through…","options":["Interest rates on loans","Pricing of goods and their usufructs","Inflation indexation of loans","Central bank policy rates"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The time factor in business transactions has value only through the pricing of goods and their usufructs (p. 13)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch1-t8"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch1-t8","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"1.9","sectionTitle":"Regulating Trade and Business","title":"Regulating Trade and Business: The Role of Government and Sound Money Creation","covers":["t1.9"],"overview":"The author argues Islam recognizes free trade and enterprise but also the need for government oversight of business, and that global financial reform requires two priorities: an active (not interventionist-in-business, but overseeing) government role, and linking money creation to real assets.","simpleExplanation":"Islam supports free trade but not a total free-for-all — government needs to actively make sure markets function fairly, without directly running businesses itself. On the money side, the author argues financial instruments should be tied to real, genuine assets and transactions rather than created out of thin air, since fictitious financial assets and unchecked risk transfer amount to a kind of fraud.","academicExplanation":"Ayub notes Islam recognizes the role of markets and individual freedom in trade/business (quoting UK Chancellor Gordon Brown on trade spreading Islam peacefully across regions), while also recognizing the risk of unregulated business harming the poor and disadvantaged. He argues economists/policymakers should focus on two areas: (1) government's role — an active (not commercial/business-running) role ensuring smooth, accountable, transparent market functioning, preventing vested-interest manipulation, resonating with newer conventional concepts like ethical finance, green funds and socially responsible investment; (2) money, banking and finance — all financial transactions/instruments should be backed by genuine assets and business transactions per fair-play, transparency and justice norms. While a literal return to the gold standard is impractical, the author proposes that Islamic finance's principle — that financial assets be based on real assets (not necessarily gold/silver) and that the time value of money is realized only through the pricing of goods/usufructs, not through interest — can serve as an equivalent disciplining criterion. He argues fictitious asset creation and unjust risk transfer amount to cheating/fraud and should be unacceptable to anyone genuinely concerned with human rights and dignity. He closes by quoting Gordon Brown's citation of a Hadith likening the Ummah to a single body, where pain in one part is felt throughout, to argue for solidarity 'beyond the Muslim world'.","examExplanation":"The author proposes two priorities for reform: (1) government must play an active oversight (not business-running) role to keep markets accountable and transparent; (2) money/finance must be tied to real assets and genuine transactions rather than created 'out of nothing' — since fictitious financial assets and undefined risk transfer amount to cheating/fraud. He frames the Islamic finance principle of asset-backing plus pricing-based (not interest-based) time value of money as a practical substitute for the now-impractical gold standard.","keyPoints":["Islam recognizes free trade and business but also the need to regulate against harm to the poor and disadvantaged from totally unregulated business.","Government's proper role is active oversight (accountability, transparency, preventing vested-interest manipulation) — not directly running businesses.","The gold standard cannot practically be restored, but the author proposes Islamic finance's asset-backing principle as an equivalent disciplining criterion for money creation.","The time value of money in Islamic finance is realized only through the pricing of goods and their usufructs (benefits/use), not through interest on money itself.","Creating financial assets 'out of nothing' and transferring undefined risk to others is characterized as cheating and fraud.","The author quotes a Hadith (via Gordon Brown) likening the Ummah to one body feeling collective pain, to argue for cross-border ethical solidarity."],"definitions":[],"conditions":["All financial assets and transactions must be represented by genuine assets and business transactions, consistent with fair play, transparency and justice."],"principles":["The time factor in business transactions has value only through the pricing of goods and their usufructs, not as an independent charge (interest) on money itself."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["The author is not calling for government to run businesses directly — the proposed active government role is regulatory/oversight, distinct from state ownership of enterprise."],"distinctions":["Asset-backed money creation (the Islamic finance principle proposed here) vs money/credit creation detached from real assets (criticized as fictitious and prone to fraud/exploitation)."],"relatedConcepts":["Time value of money","Asset-backed finance","Ummah","Regulatory oversight"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.9","pages":[13,14]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Government running businesses","b":"Government as active overseer","text":"The author wants an active state role, not to conduct business but to ensure markets function with accountability and transparency so vested interests cannot manipulate them (p. 13)."}],"principles":["All financial assets must be based on real assets.","The time factor has value only through pricing of goods and their usufructs.","Parties owning risk and reward at each stage are entitled to profit according to the nature of the transaction."]},{"id":"t1.10","section":"1.10","title":"Islamic Finance Passing Significant Milestones","pages":[15,16],"tier":"supporting","concepts":["islamic-banking","standard-setters"],"intuition":"How far had Islamic finance come by the time of writing? From an academic dream in the early 1970s to a global industry.","simpleExplanation":"In about 25 years Islamic finance grew from an idea to a global industry: practised in over 75 countries by about 550 institutions, supported by standard-setting bodies like AAOIFI and IFSB, and offered even by conventional banks and to non-Muslims.","academicExplanation":["Islamic finance passed the milestones of existence, recognition by global financial authorities and delivery of sophisticated services with competitive pricing and care for Shari’ah compliance. Until the early 1970s it was an academic dream; it made headway in the 1980s despite an unfavourable environment.","At the time of writing it was practised in over 75 countries by about 550 institutions, with multinational conventional groups offering products through subsidiaries or windows. Standard-setting bodies and facilitators — AAOIFI, IFSB, IIFM and the Liquidity Management Centre (LMC) — enhance credibility. Bahrain, Malaysia, Saudi Arabia and Dubai served as hubs, with London and Singapore aspiring to become centres."],"examExplanation":"Milestones: existence → recognition → sophisticated delivery. ~75 countries, ~550 IFIs (at time of writing). Standard setters: AAOIFI, IFSB, IIFM, LMC. Hubs: Bahrain, Malaysia, Saudi Arabia, Dubai; London and Singapore aspiring.","keyPoints":["Islamic Bank of Britain cited as a full-fledged Islamic bank in the UK.","In Malaysia about 40% of Islamic banks’ clients were non-Muslims (as cited).","Figures reflect the book’s time of writing (2007).","About 550 Islamic financial institutions in over 75 countries at the time of writing.","Hubs: Bahrain, Malaysia, Saudi Arabia and Dubai; London and Singapore competing to join them.","In Malaysia about 40% of Islamic banks’ clients are non-Muslims."],"definitions":[{"term":"AAOIFI","definition":"Accounting and Auditing Organization for Islamic Financial Institutions — a standard-setting body."},{"term":"IFSB","definition":"Islamic Financial Services Board — a standard-setting institution."}],"examples":[{"title":"Scale at the time of writing","kind":"textbook","text":"About 550 Islamic financial institutions operated in over 75 countries, with hubs in Bahrain, Malaysia, Saudi Arabia and Dubai; in Malaysia about 40 % of Islamic banks’ clients were non-Muslims (pp. 15–16)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"The figures (75 countries, 550 IFIs) are current statistics.","right":"They are the author’s figures at the time of writing (book published 2007)."}],"relatedTopics":["t8.5","t18.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which of these is listed by the author as a standard-setting body or global facilitator for Islamic finance?","options":["IFSB","OPEC","WTO","Basel Committee"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"AAOIFI, IFSB, IIFM and LMC are listed (p. 15)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch1-t9"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch1-t9","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"1.10","sectionTitle":"Islamic Finance Passing Significant Milestones","title":"Islamic Finance's Growth and Global Milestones","covers":["t1.10"],"overview":"The author documents the rapid growth and mainstream recognition of Islamic finance from an academic idea in the early 1970s to a global industry by the mid-2000s.","simpleExplanation":"Islamic banking went from being just an academic idea in the early 1970s to a real, global industry within about 25 years — with recognition from major regulators, universities and even conventional banks setting up Islamic subsidiaries, in dozens of countries including non-Muslim-majority ones like the UK.","academicExplanation":"Ayub charts Islamic finance's rise: an academic dream until the early 1970s, gaining ground in the 1980s despite an unfavourable environment and the absence of supporting institutions, reaching by the mid-2000s roughly 75+ countries and about 550 Islamic financial institutions. He cites recognition from major regulators (US Federal Reserve Board, UK FSA), international institutions (IMF, World Bank), and prestigious universities (Harvard, Rice, LSE, Loughborough, Durham, International Islamic Universities in Malaysia/Pakistan). Standard-setting bodies — AAOIFI, IFSB, IIFM and the Liquidity Management Centre (LMC) — are credited with enhancing credibility. Bahrain, Malaysia, Saudi Arabia and Dubai are named as long-standing hubs, with London and Singapore emerging as new centres (UK Chancellor Gordon Brown's stated ambition to make London a global Islamic finance centre, 2006). He gives UK-specific examples: the Islamic Bank of Britain (IBB), operating with seven branches (planned 12 by end-2007), whose first Leicester customer was a non-Muslim who travelled 100+ miles for the bank's ethics/transparency; also EIIB, HSBC Amanah, Alburaq and several professional-services firms serving non-Muslim clients too. In Malaysia, about 40% of Islamic banks' clients are non-Muslim. He frames continuing global financial instability as likely to further boost demand for the market discipline inherent in Islamic finance principles.","examExplanation":"Islamic finance grew from an academic idea (pre-1970s) to a global industry (~75 countries, ~550 institutions by the mid-2000s) within about 25 years, gaining recognition from central banks/regulators (Fed, FSA), IFIs (IMF, World Bank), universities, and standard-setters (AAOIFI, IFSB, IIFM, LMC). Traditional hubs: Bahrain, Malaysia, Saudi Arabia, Dubai; emerging hubs: London, Singapore. Non-Muslim uptake is notable (e.g. ~40% of Malaysian Islamic banks' clients are non-Muslim; UK's Islamic Bank of Britain serves non-Muslim customers too).","keyPoints":["Islamic banking moved from an academic idea (pre-1970s) to global practice in roughly 25 years, expanding significantly in the 1980s despite an unfavourable environment.","By the mid-2000s: Islamic finance operating in over 75 countries via about 550 institutions.","Key standard-setting/infrastructure bodies: AAOIFI (accounting/auditing standards), IFSB (Islamic Financial Services Board), IIFM (International Islamic Financial Market), LMC (Liquidity Management Centre).","Traditional hubs: Bahrain, Malaysia, Saudi Arabia, Dubai; London and Singapore emerging as new centres (UK Chancellor Gordon Brown's 2006 push to make London a hub).","Non-Muslim customer uptake is significant: ~40% of Malaysian Islamic banks' clients are non-Muslim; the Islamic Bank of Britain's first customer was a non-Muslim."],"definitions":[{"term":"AAOIFI","definition":"Accounting and Auditing Organization for Islamic Financial Institutions — a standard-setting body enhancing the credibility/recognition of Islamic finance."},{"term":"IFSB","definition":"Islamic Financial Services Board — a global standard-setting body for Islamic financial services."}],"conditions":[],"principles":[],"processSteps":[],"examples":[{"title":"Islamic Bank of Britain's first customer","kind":"textbook","text":"The first customer to open an account at the Leicester branch of the Islamic Bank of Britain (IBB) was a non-Muslim who travelled over 100 miles, drawn by the ethics and transparency the author says an Islamic bank offers."}],"commonConfusions":[],"distinctions":[],"relatedConcepts":["AAOIFI","IFSB","IIFM","Islamic banking hubs"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"beginner","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.10","pages":[15,16]}}]},{"id":"t1.11","section":"1.11","title":"Could it Work to Achieve the Objectives?","pages":[16,17],"tier":"core","concepts":["form-vs-substance"],"intuition":"Is it enough for a product to be legally Shari’ah compliant? The author warns that formal compliance without changed substance undermines the system.","simpleExplanation":"Islamic finance can only achieve its goals if it changes mindsets and substance, not just legal forms. Copying conventional products and dressing them up to fit Islamic rules undermines the theory.","academicExplanation":["Selective and half-hearted application by individual IFIs, without State-level R&D and implementation by policymakers, regulators and central monetary bodies, may not provide a better alternative. The system must be developed not only to be legally or formally Shari’ah compliant but to change its basis and the mindset of stakeholders to realise the objectives of the pioneers.","The author’s message: IFIs that mould conventional products to fit formally into the Islamic perspective, without regard to their impact on society, undermine the integrity of Islamic finance. Compliance should be “Shari’ah inspired”. Good ethics are also needed, and Islamic countries should set the precedent, replacing borrowed capital inflows with direct and portfolio investment, with the State protecting society from vested interests."],"examExplanation":"Success requires State-level commitment, R&D, ethics and Shari’ah-inspired (not merely formal/legal) compliance; mimicking conventional products undermines the integrity of Islamic finance.","keyPoints":["Formal compliance is not enough — compliance should be Shari’ah inspired.","State-level R&D and application needed.","Ethics must be taught and enforced.","The State must protect the masses from rogue elements."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Form versus substance","kind":"practice","text":"Bank A renames its interest-bearing loan “Murabaha” but never buys the goods. Bank B buys the goods, takes their risk and sells them at a fixed price. Both products look alike on paper; only B meets the “Shari’ah-inspired” standard the author calls for (pp. 16–17)."}],"commonConfusions":[{"wrong":"If a product is legally Shari’ah compliant, Islamic finance has achieved its objectives.","right":"The author warns that copying conventional products and fitting them formally undermines the theory; compliance must be Shari’ah-inspired and consider the impact on society (pp. 16–17)."}],"relatedTopics":["t17.4","t18.3"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"What does the author say about IFIs that adapt conventional products merely to fit Islamic legal forms?","options":["They represent best practice","They undermine the integrity of Islamic finance theory","They are required by AAOIFI","They eliminate all risk"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"He warns that such moulding without regard to impact undermines the integrity of the theory (p. 16)."},"origin":["f1","f2"],"aliases":["ch1-t10"],"supplements":[{"id":"ch1-t10","origin":"f2","sectionRange":"1.11","sectionTitle":"Could it Work to Achieve the Objectives?","title":"Could Islamic Finance Achieve Its Objectives? — Outlook and the Book's Roadmap","covers":["t1.11"],"overview":"The author reflects on whether Islamic economics/finance can realistically become a genuine alternative, cautions against merely 'form-compliant' Islamic finance, and closes by outlining the book's three-part structure.","simpleExplanation":"The author is hopeful but cautious: Islamic finance can only really deliver on its promise if it changes the underlying mindset of practitioners — not just tweak conventional products to formally 'look' Shari'ah-compliant. Real ethics, genuine risk-sharing, and serious government commitment are needed, not shortcuts. The book itself is organized in three parts: fundamentals and prohibitions (Part I), contract law (Part II), and the specific financing products, capital markets, Takaful and criticism/way forward (Part III).","academicExplanation":"Ayub poses the central question of whether Islamic economics/finance can eventually provide a genuinely better global alternative, noting this requires State-level research & development and serious application, not just selective/half-hearted use by individual Islamic financial institutions (IFIs). He argues that if the majority of the world's (then) 1.2 billion Muslims strictly abided by the Shari'ah prohibition on interest, much of global commerce could shift toward fair-play-based instruments, and cooperation with human-rights-oriented groups could help resolve issues like hunger and poverty. However, he cautions that the system must be developed to be substantively (not merely formally/legally) Shari'ah compliant — criticizing IFIs that merely reshape conventional products to superficially 'fit' the Islamic label without regard to their impact on society, arguing this undermines the integrity of Islamic finance theory; compliance should be 'Shari'ah inspired' as the foremost consideration for product developers, implementers and regulators. He stresses good ethics (currently lacking broadly, including in Islamic countries) must be actively cultivated by governments, academics and opinion-makers. In 1.12 ('About This Book'), he frames Islamic finance as the most effective operating part of the broader Islamic economic system (not the whole of it), states the book represents the mainstream view of Islamic finance (as used by AAOIFI/IFSB and practiced in the Middle East and elsewhere), and lays out the book's structure: Part I (this chapter's part) covers the need for, basis and structure of Islamic economics and the main prohibitions IFIs must observe (with Riba receiving the most attention as the least controversial prohibition); Part II (3 chapters) covers Islamic contract law — sale, loans/debts, and related subcontracts (Hawalah, Kafalah, Rahn); Part III (the largest, most strategic part) covers financial products and modes — Murabaha/Mu'ajjal, Salam, Istisna'a, Ijarah, Musharakah, Mudarabah, Diminishing Musharakah, accessory contracts (Wakalah, Tawarruq, Ju'alah, Istijrar), application/deposit-and-financing principles, Sukuk/securitization, Takaful, an appraisal of common criticism (Ch.17), and the book's concluding 'Way Forward' (Ch.18).","examExplanation":"The author's forward-looking caution: Islamic finance's promise depends on substantive (not merely formal/legal) Shari'ah compliance, genuine mindset change among practitioners, and serious State-level commitment — not selective or superficial application by individual IFIs reshaping conventional products to 'formally fit' Islamic labels. The book's own structure: Part I = fundamentals/prohibitions (Ch.1-4); Part II = Islamic contract law (Ch.5-7); Part III = financing modes, products, capital markets, Takaful, criticism and the way forward (Ch.8-18).","keyPoints":["Realizing Islamic finance's full potential requires State-level research & development and serious application, not just selective use by individual IFIs.","The author cautions against IFIs that merely reshape conventional products to formally/legally 'fit' Islamic labels without regard for their real socio-economic impact — this undermines the integrity of Islamic finance theory.","Shari'ah compliance should be substantively 'Shari'ah inspired', not just formally/legally compliant.","The book represents the mainstream view of Islamic finance, consistent with AAOIFI/IFSB standards and Middle East practice.","Book structure: Part I = fundamentals and prohibitions (Ch.1-4, with Riba given the most attention); Part II = Islamic contract law (Ch.5-7: sale, loans/debts, subcontracts like Hawalah/Kafalah/Rahn); Part III = financing modes and products (Ch.8-16: Murabaha, Salam/Istisna'a, Ijarah, Musharakah/Mudarabah, accessory contracts, application principles, Sukuk, Takaful), criticism appraisal (Ch.17) and the way forward (Ch.18)."],"definitions":[],"conditions":[],"principles":["Compliance with Islamic finance principles should be substantively 'Shari'ah inspired' rather than merely formally/legally Shari'ah compliant."],"processSteps":[],"examples":[],"commonConfusions":["'Legally/formally Shari'ah compliant' and 'Shari'ah inspired/substantively compliant' are not the same thing in the author's framing — a product can pass formal contract checks while, in the author's view, failing the deeper socio-economic purpose of Islamic finance."],"distinctions":["Formal/legal Shari'ah compliance (contract mechanics only) vs substantive/'Shari'ah inspired' compliance (also considering real socio-economic impact) — a recurring theme the author returns to throughout the book."],"relatedConcepts":["Shari'ah compliance","AAOIFI","IFSB","Islamic economic system"],"examRelevance":"supporting","difficulty":"intermediate","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.11","pages":[16,17,18,19]}}],"distinctions":[{"a":"Formal/legal compliance","b":"Shari’ah-inspired compliance","text":"The first fits a product to legal forms; the second also considers the product’s basis and its impact on society."}]},{"id":"t1.12","section":"1.12","title":"About this Book","pages":[17,19],"tier":"revision","concepts":[],"intuition":"How is the book organised, and whose interpretation does it follow? It follows the mainstream view and is built in three parts.","simpleExplanation":"The book follows the mainstream Shari’ah view (also used by AAOIFI and IFSB). Part I covers foundations and prohibitions, Part II the law of contracts, trading and debt, and Part III products, procedures, Sukuk, Takaful, criticism and the way forward.","academicExplanation":["Islamic finance is only one — though the most effective — part of the Islamic economic system; the banking and financial system works like blood circulating in the human body. The book represents the mainstream view of Shari’ah interpretation because of its wide recognition and because it underlies practice in the Middle East and elsewhere; standards by AAOIFI and IFSB are also based on it. Minority-view practices are described as being in transformation towards the mainstream.","Part I: need, basis and structure of Islamic economics; philosophy and features of Islamic finance; major prohibitions (with emphasis on Riba). Part II: law of contracts, trading, loans and debts, and subcontracts such as Hawalah, Kafalah and Rahn. Part III: conventional and Islamic products; Murabaha–Mu‘ajjal, Salam, Istisna‘a, Ijarah, Musharakah, Mudarabah, Diminishing Musharakah; accessory contracts (Wakalah, Tawarruq, Ju‘alah, Istijrar); application principles; Sukuk; Takaful; appraisal of criticism; and the way forward."],"examExplanation":"Mainstream view followed (basis of AAOIFI/IFSB standards). Part I Fundamentals (Ch 1–4); Part II Contractual bases (Ch 5–7); Part III Products and procedures (Ch 8–18).","keyPoints":["Prohibition of Riba is described as the least controversial issue.","Conventional banks’ main function is loaning; Islamic banks’ activities pertain to trading, leasing and real-sector business.","Chapter 17 was added to appraise criticism.","Part I: foundations and prohibitions; Part II: law of contracts; Part III: products and modes, plus Sukuk, Takaful, criticism and the way forward."],"definitions":[],"examples":[{"title":"Map of the book","kind":"textbook","text":"Part I: foundations and prohibitions; Part II: law of contracts, trading, loans and debts (with Hawalah, Kafalah, Rahn); Part III: products, procedures, Sukuk, Takaful, criticism and the way forward (pp. 17–19)."}],"commonConfusions":[],"relatedTopics":["t2.1","t5.1","t8.1"],"quickCheck":{"prompt":"Which interpretive approach does the book follow?","options":["Minority view only","Mainstream view","No Shari’ah view","A new interpretation by the author"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The book represents the mainstream view, which also underlies AAOIFI and IFSB standards (p. 17)."},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[],"supplements":[],"distinctions":[{"a":"Mainstream view","b":"Minority view","text":"The book follows the mainstream interpretation because it is widely recognised, underpins Middle-East practice and the AAOIFI/IFSB standards; minority-view practices are converging towards it (p. 17)."}]}],"questions":[{"id":"q1.1","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to Chapter 1, what is the “main culprit” behind the failure of the global economic system to ensure justice?","options":["Technological change","The functioning of money and finance, especially interest, along with poor economic management","Excessive regulation of banks","Religious belief"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Section 1.5 names inefficient economic management and the functioning of money/finance — particularly the institution of interest.","topicId":"t1.5","chapter":1,"section":"1.5","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify the author's diagnosis of the global economy","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.5","pages":[8,9]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.2","type":"tf","prompt":"The author argues that market mechanisms and private property have no place in Islamic economics.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Section 1.8 says market mechanisms, private property, enterprise, self-interest and competition are integral parts of Islamic economics.","topicId":"t1.8","chapter":1,"section":"1.8","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe the role of markets in Islamic economics","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[11,13]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.3","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of the following are among the four sources of problems identified in Section 1.8? (Select all that apply.)","options":["Unchecked creation of money","Reliance on market forces without ethical limits","Emphasis on growth and profit per se without regard to distribution","Excessive Zakat collection","Negative role of the State and regulators"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,4],"explanation":"The four sources are unchecked money creation, markets without ethics, growth without distribution, and the negative role of the State.","topicId":"t1.8","chapter":1,"section":"1.8","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"List the author's four problem sources","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[11,13]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.4","type":"match","prompt":"Match each contract to its category in the author’s classification.","pairs":[["Sale","Commutative — return valid"],["Leasing","Commutative — return valid"],["Loan","Gratuitous — no return"],["Guarantee","Gratuitous — no return"]],"explanation":"Sale and leasing are commutative (Uqood-e-Mu‘awadha); loans, guarantees and gifts are noncommutative/gratuitous.","topicId":"t1.8","chapter":1,"section":"1.8","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Classify contracts as commutative or gratuitous","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[11,13]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.5","type":"definition","prompt":"“A thought based upon an ideology, relating to management of wealth distribution in a society.” This defines:","options":["Economic science","An economic system","Monetary policy","Islamic banking"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Section 1.7 distinguishes economic science (creation of wealth) from an economic system (ideology-based, wealth distribution).","topicId":"t1.7","chapter":1,"section":"1.7","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Distinguish economic science from an economic system","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.7","pages":[10,11]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.6","type":"scenario","prompt":"A government reduces its external debt but borrows heavily at high interest from domestic banks. Based on Section 1.2, how would the author assess this?","options":["The debt problem is solved","Declines in external debt offset by rising domestic debt still expose the country to risk from the overall debt burden","Domestic debt carries no cost","This is an example of risk-sharing"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"The author notes aggregate declines in external indebtedness have been offset by rises in domestic debt, exposing countries to enhanced risk.","topicId":"t1.2","chapter":1,"section":"1.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the author's critique of debt","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.2","pages":[4,6]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.7","type":"identify","prompt":"Which principle is described as “the basic juristic principle of Shari’ah and a normative rule of justice”?","options":["Time value of money","No risk, no gain","Freedom of contract","Caveat emptor"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Section 1.5: “No risk, no gain” is the basic juristic principle.","topicId":"t1.5","chapter":1,"section":"1.5","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the risk–return principle","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.5","pages":[8,9]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.8","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which two principles does the author say provide the best criteria for the creation of money?","options":["Gold backing and fixed exchange rates","All financial assets based on real assets; time has value only through pricing of goods and usufructs","Interest-rate targeting and inflation control","Free capital flows and deregulation"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Section 1.9 states these two principles.","topicId":"t1.9","chapter":1,"section":"1.9","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain asset-backing and time value in Islamic finance","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.9","pages":[13,14]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.9","type":"order","prompt":"Put the three parts of the book in order.","items":["Fundamentals","Contractual Bases in Islamic Finance","Islamic Finance – Products and Procedures"],"explanation":"Part I Fundamentals (Ch 1–4), Part II Contractual Bases (Ch 5–7), Part III Products and Procedures (Ch 8–18).","topicId":"t1.12","chapter":1,"section":"1.12","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Outline the structure of the book","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.12","pages":[17,19]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.10","type":"short","prompt":"In two or three sentences, explain why the author warns against merely formal Shari’ah compliance.","keywords":["formal","impact","integrity","inspired","conventional"],"correctAnswer":"Products moulded from conventional ones to fit Islamic legal forms, without regard to their impact on society, undermine the integrity of Islamic finance. The system must change its basis and stakeholders’ mindset; compliance should be Shari’ah inspired to realise the pioneers’ objectives.","explanation":"See Section 1.11.","topicId":"t1.11","chapter":1,"section":"1.11","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Evaluate formal versus substantive compliance","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.11","pages":[16,17]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.11","type":"tf","prompt":"The author states that the gold standard should be re-adopted as the basis of money creation.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"He says the gold standard cannot be adopted again, but there must be foolproof criteria for money creation; assets need not be gold or silver.","topicId":"t1.9","chapter":1,"section":"1.9","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain asset-backing","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.9","pages":[13,14]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.12","type":"application","prompt":"A guarantor offers to guarantee a company’s bank financing in return for a fee of 1% of the amount guaranteed. According to the footnote in Section 1.8, this fee:","options":["Is permissible as a commutative contract","Involves Riba; only out-of-pocket expenses may be recovered","Is permissible if agreed in writing","Is Zakat"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Guaranteeing a financial obligation for a price involves Riba; the guarantor may recover out-of-pocket expenses only (citing the Islamic Fiqh Academy).","topicId":"t1.8","chapter":1,"section":"1.8","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the rule on guarantees","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[11,13]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.13","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to the author, what happened to the gold–currency link in 1971?","options":["It was strengthened at Bretton Woods","It was abolished, and gold became an ordinary commodity","It was replaced by a link to the Pound Sterling","It was restored at $35 per ounce"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Bretton Woods (1944) fixed $35 per ounce; in 1971 the link was abolished (p. 3, n. 1).","topicId":"t1.1","chapter":1,"section":"1.1","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the history of the monetary system","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.1","pages":[3,4]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.14","type":"tf","prompt":"The author quotes Keynes to support the view that enlightened self-interest always operates in the public interest.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Keynes is quoted for the opposite: private and social interests do not always coincide (p. 4).","topicId":"t1.1","chapter":1,"section":"1.1","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Interpret the Keynes quotation","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.1","pages":[3,4]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.15","type":"multi","prompt":"Which of these does the author list as consequences of the interest-based debt mechanism? (Select all.)","options":["Governments raise taxes on consumption to service debt","An increasing share of risk-free capital relative to risk-based capital","Higher national savings","Concentration of wealth in fewer hands","Foreign-exchange earnings consumed by debt servicing"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,3,4],"explanation":"Savings decline, not rise, because easy credit promotes wasteful spending (pp. 4–5).","topicId":"t1.2","chapter":1,"section":"1.2","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain how interest-based debt harms economies","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.2","pages":[4,6]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.16","type":"application","prompt":"A country’s GDP grows at 9% a year, yet rural poverty worsens and its Gini coefficient rises. Which argument from Chapter 1 best explains this?","options":["Growth benefits trickle down automatically","Growth per se does not lead to socio-economic justice when assets are concentrated","Inflation always falls with growth","Trade barriers are the only cause"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Section 1.3: where wealth is concentrated, even high growth does not improve distribution — the China example (p. 7).","topicId":"t1.3","chapter":1,"section":"1.3","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the growth-versus-justice argument","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.3","pages":[6,8]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.17","type":"mcq","prompt":"What remedy does the author propose for mobilising funds in place of borrowed money?","options":["More concessional loans","Replacing risk-free with risk-related capital, including direct and portfolio investment","Printing more reserve currency","Higher interest rates to attract savings"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 8: borrowed funds are mostly squandered; replace them with asset- and risk-based investment.","topicId":"t1.3","chapter":1,"section":"1.3","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the proposed funding solution","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.3","pages":[6,8]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.18","type":"tf","prompt":"The author believes large charitable foundations and state welfare spending can, on their own, eliminate poverty created by the financial system.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Selective spending cannot correct system-wide imbalances; the system itself must change (p. 8, including n. 15 on “Billanthropy”).","topicId":"t1.4","chapter":1,"section":"1.4","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Evaluate the role of welfare spending","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.4","pages":[8,8]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.19","type":"identify","prompt":"Which two factors does the author name as the MAIN causes of the global system’s failure?","options":["Population growth and wars","Inefficient economic management unconcerned with poverty, and the functioning of money and financial markets","Religion and culture","Technology and automation"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Section 1.5 (p. 8).","topicId":"t1.5","chapter":1,"section":"1.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Identify the main culprits","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.5","pages":[8,9]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.20","type":"comparison","prompt":"How does loss liability change investor behaviour, according to Section 1.5?","options":["It has no effect","Bearing possible loss makes investors more careful and injects discipline, removing the moral hazard of risk-free gains","It makes investors reckless","It guarantees profits"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 9.","topicId":"t1.5","chapter":1,"section":"1.5","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Analyse the discipline effect of risk-sharing","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.5","pages":[8,9]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.21","type":"mcq","prompt":"In the author’s view, what is the raison d’être of any economic system that is to be sustainable?","options":["Maximum growth","Justice / fair play","Low inflation","Free trade"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 9: justice cannot be given up for any other consideration in the Islamic worldview.","topicId":"t1.6","chapter":1,"section":"1.6","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the core objective of the system","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.6","pages":[9,10]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.22","type":"order","prompt":"Order the stages of Malaysia’s experience described in Section 1.7.","items":["Trickle-down growth policy (1957–1970)","Race riots of 13 May 1969","New Economic Policy: growth with distributive justice","Significant achievement over the following three decades"],"explanation":"p. 11.","topicId":"t1.7","chapter":1,"section":"1.7","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Sequence the Malaysian case","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.7","pages":[10,11]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.23","type":"tf","prompt":"According to the author, market mechanisms, private property, self-interest and competition are integral parts of Islamic economics.","correctAnswer":true,"explanation":"p. 12 — they operate within ethical limits and state oversight.","topicId":"t1.8","chapter":1,"section":"1.8","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the role of markets in Islamic economics","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[11,13]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.24","type":"scenario","prompt":"A farmer harvests wheat above the Zakat threshold. Under the rates mentioned in Chapter 1, which applies?","options":["2.5% of net wealth","5% or 10% of the produce","No Zakat on produce","20% of the harvest"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"Zakat: generally 2.5% of net wealth, or 5% or 10% for agricultural produce above a minimum (p. 12).","topicId":"t1.8","chapter":1,"section":"1.8","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply Zakat rates","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[11,13]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.25","type":"match","prompt":"Match each contract to whether a return may be charged.","pairs":[["Sale","Profit permitted (commutative)"],["Lease","Rent permitted (commutative)"],["Loan","No return — gratuitous"],["Guarantee","No fee — only out-of-pocket expenses"]],"explanation":"Uqood-e-Mu‘awadha vs Uqood Ghair Mu‘awadha (pp. 12–13, n. 20).","topicId":"t1.8","chapter":1,"section":"1.8","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Classify contracts by permissibility of return","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[11,13]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.26","type":"short","prompt":"What happens to a debtor’s liability if he becomes insolvent, according to Chapter 1?","keywords":["repaid","insolvent","Hereafter","waive"],"correctAnswer":"The loan still has to be repaid one way or another until the creditor relaxes it or the debtor is declared insolvent; even then, accountability for the debt in the Hereafter remains until the creditor waives it.","explanation":"p. 13.","topicId":"t1.8","chapter":1,"section":"1.8","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the accountability of debt","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[11,13]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.27","type":"mcq","prompt":"Which analogy does the author use for the financial system?","options":["A machine","The heart in the human body / blood circulation","A marketplace","A river"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"pp. 14 and 17: the heart, and money as circulating blood.","topicId":"t1.9","chapter":1,"section":"1.9","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the author’s analogy","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.9","pages":[13,14]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.28","type":"application","prompt":"A regulator wants to stop banks creating financial assets “out of nothing”. Which principle from Section 1.9 addresses this?","options":["All financial assets must be based on real assets and business activities","All money must be gold","Interest must be capped at 5%","Banks must hold 100% reserves in Dollars"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"pp. 13–14: creating assets out of nothing and shifting undefined risk is tantamount to cheating.","topicId":"t1.9","chapter":1,"section":"1.9","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Apply the real-asset principle","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.9","pages":[13,14]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.29","type":"identify","prompt":"Which of these is a standard-setting or facilitating body named in Chapter 1?","options":["IFSB","OPEC","WTO","SWIFT"],"correctAnswer":0,"explanation":"AAOIFI, IFSB, IIFM and LMC (p. 15).","topicId":"t1.10","chapter":1,"section":"1.10","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall Islamic finance infrastructure bodies","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.10","pages":[15,16]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.30","type":"tf","prompt":"The author claims that Islamic banking was already widely practised by the early 1970s.","correctAnswer":false,"explanation":"Until the early 1970s it was an academic dream; it made headway in the 1980s (p. 15).","topicId":"t1.10","chapter":1,"section":"1.10","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Recall the growth timeline","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.10","pages":[15,16]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q1.31","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the book follow the mainstream interpretation of Shari’ah?","options":["It is the only valid view","It is widely recognised, underlies Middle-East practice and the AAOIFI/IFSB standards","It is the most lenient","It was chosen by the publisher"],"correctAnswer":1,"explanation":"p. 17.","topicId":"t1.12","chapter":1,"section":"1.12","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the book’s interpretive stance","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.12","pages":[17,19]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"q-ch1-1","type":"mcq","prompt":"According to Ayub, what event in 1971 significantly changed the global monetary system established at Bretton Woods?","explanation":"In 1971 the gold-currency link established at Bretton Woods (1944) was abolished; gold became a normal commodity priced in currency rather than the basis for currency valuation.","topicId":"t1.1","chapter":1,"section":"1.1","topicLabel":"Failures of the Neoclassical/Capitalist Economic Scenario","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain why the author sees the interest-based conventional financial system as a source of exploitation and inequitable growth","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.1","pages":[3,4]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The gold-currency link was abolished","The IMF was created","The US Dollar was pegged to the Euro","Communism officially collapsed"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch1-2","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: Ayub argues that Keynes believed private and social interests always coincide under enlightened self-interest.","explanation":"Ayub quotes Keynes as saying the opposite: the world is not so governed that private and social interests always coincide, and self-interest is often not enlightened enough to serve the public interest.","topicId":"t1.1","chapter":1,"section":"1.1","topicLabel":"Failures of the Neoclassical/Capitalist Economic Scenario","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain why the author sees the interest-based conventional financial system as a source of exploitation and inequitable growth","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.1","pages":[3,4]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch1-3","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why does Ayub describe conventional interest-based debt as self-perpetuating?","explanation":"Ayub states that excessive debt and its servicing are hallmarks of the interest-based mechanism, where yesterday's debt is repaid by taking out more debt today rather than being genuinely settled.","topicId":"t1.2","chapter":1,"section":"1.2","topicLabel":"Conventional Debt as a Recipe for Exploitation","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe the link the author draws between excessive debt, unchecked money creation and global financial instability","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.2","pages":[4,5]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because governments prefer to keep some debt permanently outstanding for tax reasons","Because yesterday's debt is typically repaid by taking on more debt today, rather than genuinely being paid off","Because interest rates always decline over time","Because the IMF requires countries to remain indebted"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch1-4","type":"short","prompt":"What US national debt figure does Ayub cite as having been passed by 2006, and what nickname does he quote for it?","explanation":"The chapter states American national debt passed $9 trillion, quoting the late columnist Art Buchwald's description of it as 'The $9-trillion heist'.","topicId":"t1.2","chapter":1,"section":"1.2","topicLabel":"Conventional Debt as a Recipe for Exploitation","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Describe the link the author draws between excessive debt, unchecked money creation and global financial instability","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.2","pages":[5]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Over $9 trillion; columnist Art Buchwald termed it 'The $9-trillion heist'."},{"id":"q-ch1-5","type":"mcq","prompt":"Why, according to Ayub, has the US been relatively immune to the devastation of its own debt burden?","explanation":"Ayub attributes US immunity from debt devastation specifically to the Dollar being the major reserve currency, despite the US being a zero-saving nation with unparalleled debt.","topicId":"t1.2","chapter":1,"section":"1.2","topicLabel":"Conventional Debt as a Recipe for Exploitation","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe the link the author draws between excessive debt, unchecked money creation and global financial instability","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.2","pages":[5,6]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Because it has the highest savings rate in the world","Because of the US Dollar's status as the world's major reserve currency","Because the IMF forgave all US debt","Because US debt is entirely owed to domestic creditors"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch1-6","type":"scenario","prompt":"A country posts double-digit GDP growth for a decade, but rural poverty worsens because prior state guarantees of food, healthcare and education collapse under market transition, while urban big-business wealth concentrates further. Which example does Ayub use to illustrate exactly this dynamic?","explanation":"Ayub cites China as an example of high growth coinciding with worsening rural poverty, as prior communist-era welfare guarantees collapsed under the shift to a market-based system.","topicId":"t1.3","chapter":1,"section":"1.3","topicLabel":"Growth Without Distributive Justice, and the Limits of State Welfare Spending","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Summarize why growth alone (without distributive justice) fails to reduce poverty","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.3","pages":[6,7]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The United States after 1970","China","Malaysia after 1970","Post-Bretton-Woods Britain"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch1-7","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does 'trickle-down theory' (TDT) claim, and what does Ayub say happens in practice?","explanation":"TDT holds that growth benefiting the powerful eventually trickles down to the masses; Ayub argues it instead widens asset-distribution inequality by letting the already powerful grow even faster.","topicId":"t1.3","chapter":1,"section":"1.3","topicLabel":"Growth Without Distributive Justice, and the Limits of State Welfare Spending","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Summarize why growth alone (without distributive justice) fails to reduce poverty","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.3","pages":[6,7]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["It claims growth benefits the powerful first and then spreads to everyone; Ayub says it actually widens inequality","It claims government spending always reduces poverty; Ayub agrees fully","It claims poverty is caused only by low growth; Ayub disputes this","It claims Islamic economics rejects all growth; Ayub disputes this"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch1-8","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: According to Ayub, government social-welfare spending alone is sufficient to correct the injustice inflicted by the conventional economic system.","explanation":"Ayub argues such spending cannot mitigate the ill effects of the system as a whole, can create moral hazard, and cannot reach the scale of need — structural change at the production/distribution stage is required.","topicId":"t1.3","chapter":1,"section":"1.3","topicLabel":"Growth Without Distributive Justice, and the Limits of State Welfare Spending","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Summarize why growth alone (without distributive justice) fails to reduce poverty","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.3","pages":[8]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch1-9","type":"short","prompt":"State the 'no risk, no gain' principle as Ayub presents it.","explanation":"This is the core normative principle the author introduces to justify prohibiting interest: entitlement to return requires bearing corresponding risk of loss.","topicId":"t1.5","chapter":1,"section":"1.5","topicLabel":"Interest (Riba) as the Main Culprit and the 'No Risk, No Gain' Principle","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"State the 'no risk, no gain' principle and why it is presented as Shari'ah's normative rule of justice","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.5","pages":[8,9]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"definition","correctAnswer":"There can be no legitimate gain without risk-sharing — whoever wants a return must also be liable for the possible loss; this is presented as the basic juristic principle of Shari'ah and a normative rule of justice."},{"id":"q-ch1-10","type":"mcq","prompt":"Ayub quotes Yusuf Ali's commentary on Qur'an 2:275 to contrast trade with usury. What does this commentary say usury/interest encourages?","explanation":"The commentary contrasts legitimate trade/industry (which increases prosperity and stability) with dependence on usury, which it says encourages 'a race of idlers, cruel bloodsuckers and worthless fellows'.","topicId":"t1.5","chapter":1,"section":"1.5","topicLabel":"Interest (Riba) as the Main Culprit and the 'No Risk, No Gain' Principle","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"State the 'no risk, no gain' principle and why it is presented as Shari'ah's normative rule of justice","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.5","pages":[8,9]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Hardworking entrepreneurs and innovation","A race of idlers, cruel bloodsuckers and worthless fellows who do not know their own good","Fair and transparent pricing","Reduced government spending"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch1-11","type":"mcq","prompt":"What benefit does Ayub attribute to requiring investors to bear liability for possible loss (rather than earning risk-free interest)?","explanation":"Ayub argues that liability for loss motivates investors to be more careful and removes the moral hazard associated with risk-free financial gains, thereby injecting greater discipline into the financial system.","topicId":"t1.5","chapter":1,"section":"1.5","topicLabel":"Interest (Riba) as the Main Culprit and the 'No Risk, No Gain' Principle","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"State the 'no risk, no gain' principle and why it is presented as Shari'ah's normative rule of justice","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.5","pages":[8,9]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["It guarantees higher returns for all investors","It motivates more careful investment decisions and removes the moral hazard of risk-free gains, injecting discipline into the financial system","It eliminates all forms of business risk entirely","It allows unlimited money creation without consequence"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch1-12","type":"mcq","prompt":"What do a majority of Muslim jurists and scholars, per Ayub, identify as the ultimate cause of recurring financial crises?","explanation":"Ayub states that a vast majority of Muslim jurists and scholars believe the ultimate cause of financial crises lies in disregarding the prohibition of interest.","topicId":"t1.6","chapter":1,"section":"1.6","topicLabel":"The Need of the Hour: Comprehensive Financial Reform Based on Risk-Sharing","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain why the author sees the interest-based conventional financial system as a source of exploitation and inequitable growth","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.6","pages":[9,10]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Excessive government regulation","Disregarding the prohibition of interest","Insufficient economic growth","The abolition of the gold standard alone"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch1-13","type":"multi","prompt":"Which three elements does Ayub say comprise any economic system?","explanation":"Ayub defines an economic system as comprising: ownership of property/commodities/wealth, disposal of ownership, and distribution of wealth among people.","topicId":"t1.7","chapter":1,"section":"1.7","topicLabel":"Economics and Religion: The Elements of an Economic System","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Outline the main elements the author identifies as needed for Islamic principles to correct conventional economics","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.7","pages":[10,11]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Ownership of property, commodities and wealth","Interest rate policy","Disposal of ownership","Distribution of wealth among the people","Central bank independence"],"correctAnswer":[0,2,3]},{"id":"q-ch1-14","type":"scenario","prompt":"An economist argues 'economics' and 'an economic system' mean the same thing. Using Ayub's framework, how would you correct this?","explanation":"Ayub explicitly distinguishes economics as a science (wealth creation) from an economic system (a thought/ideology-based framework for wealth ownership, use and distribution).","topicId":"t1.7","chapter":1,"section":"1.7","topicLabel":"Economics and Religion: The Elements of an Economic System","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Outline the main elements the author identifies as needed for Islamic principles to correct conventional economics","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.7","pages":[10,11]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["They are the same; no correction needed","Economics is a science dealing with wealth creation; an economic system is an ideology-based framework governing how wealth is owned, used and distributed","Economics deals only with religion; economic systems deal only with markets","An economic system is a subset of accounting, unrelated to economics"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch1-15","type":"short","prompt":"What event in Malaysia in 1969 does Ayub link to the failure of trickle-down theory, and what policy replaced it?","explanation":"Malaysia's 1957-1970 TDT-based policy failed and contributed to the 13th May 1969 race riots, prompting a shift to the New Economic Policy focused on justice with fairness.","topicId":"t1.7","chapter":1,"section":"1.7","topicLabel":"Economics and Religion: The Elements of an Economic System","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Outline the main elements the author identifies as needed for Islamic principles to correct conventional economics","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.7","pages":[10,11]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"The 13th May 1969 race riots; replaced by the New Economic Policy, which applied the core Islamic value of justice with fairness (though not full Shari'ah)."},{"id":"q-ch1-16","type":"multi","prompt":"Which four root problems does Ayub identify as causing conventional economics' failures?","explanation":"Ayub lists exactly these four factors as the root problems that Islamic principles are meant to correct.","topicId":"t1.8","chapter":1,"section":"1.8","topicLabel":"Islamic Principles Can Make the Difference: Trade, Risk-Bearing and the Basis of Entitlement to Profit","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Outline the main elements the author identifies as needed for Islamic principles to correct conventional economics","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[11,12]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Unchecked creation of money","Reliance on market forces without ethical limits","Emphasis on growth/profit without regard to distribution","The negative/passive role of the State and regulators in allowing unchecked greed","Excessive religious regulation of markets"],"correctAnswer":[0,1,2,3]},{"id":"q-ch1-17","type":"comparison","prompt":"Contrast commutative (Uqood-e-Mu'awadha) and noncommutative (Uqood Ghair Mu'awadha) contracts, with an example of each, per Chapter 1.","explanation":"Chapter 1 defines commutative contracts (sale, leasing) as those where profit is valid due to mutual consideration, while noncommutative contracts (gifts, guarantees, loans) are gratuitous/benevolent, permitting no return.","topicId":"t1.8","chapter":1,"section":"1.8","topicLabel":"Islamic Principles Can Make the Difference: Trade, Risk-Bearing and the Basis of Entitlement to Profit","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Outline the main elements the author identifies as needed for Islamic principles to correct conventional economics","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[12,13]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Commutative contracts (e.g. sale, leasing) involve consideration on both sides and permit profit; noncommutative contracts (e.g. gifts, guarantees, loans) are gratuitous/benevolent acts permitting no return","Commutative contracts always involve interest; noncommutative contracts never involve any consideration","There is no meaningful difference; both permit profit equally","Noncommutative contracts (sale, leasing) permit profit; commutative contracts (gifts, loans) do not"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch1-18","type":"mcq","prompt":"At what rate is Zakat generally levied on net wealth, per Chapter 1?","explanation":"Zakat is generally levied at 2.5% of net wealth above the Nisab threshold (with different rates, 5% or 10%, for qualifying agricultural produce).","topicId":"t1.8","chapter":1,"section":"1.8","topicLabel":"Islamic Principles Can Make the Difference: Trade, Risk-Bearing and the Basis of Entitlement to Profit","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Outline the main elements the author identifies as needed for Islamic principles to correct conventional economics","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[11,12]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["1%","2.5%","10%","20%"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch1-19","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: A guarantor (in Kafalah) may charge a fee reflecting the opportunity cost of funds set aside to cover the guarantee.","explanation":"Per the author's footnote, charging for a guarantee involves Riba and is prohibited; the guarantor may recover only actual out-of-pocket expenses, not an opportunity-cost fee.","topicId":"t1.8","chapter":1,"section":"1.8","topicLabel":"Islamic Principles Can Make the Difference: Trade, Risk-Bearing and the Basis of Entitlement to Profit","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Outline the main elements the author identifies as needed for Islamic principles to correct conventional economics","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[12,13]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch1-20","type":"mcq","prompt":"How does Ayub say the time value of money is realized in Islamic finance?","explanation":"Ayub states the time factor in business transactions has value only through the pricing of goods and their usufructs, not as an independent interest charge on money.","topicId":"t1.9","chapter":1,"section":"1.9","topicLabel":"Regulating Trade and Business: The Role of Government and Sound Money Creation","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Outline the main elements the author identifies as needed for Islamic principles to correct conventional economics","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.9","pages":[13,14]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Through a fixed annual interest rate set by central banks","Only through the pricing of goods and their usufructs (benefits/use) in genuine trading/leasing transactions","Through inflation-indexed loans","Time has no value in Islamic finance"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch1-21","type":"scenario","prompt":"A financial institution creates complex derivative instruments with no link to any real underlying asset and transfers undefined risk to unsuspecting counterparties. How would Ayub characterize this practice, based on Section 1.9?","explanation":"Ayub explicitly states that creating financial assets out of nothing and putting others at undefined risk is tantamount to cheating and fraud, and that all financial assets must be based on real assets and business activities.","topicId":"t1.9","chapter":1,"section":"1.9","topicLabel":"Regulating Trade and Business: The Role of Government and Sound Money Creation","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Outline the main elements the author identifies as needed for Islamic principles to correct conventional economics","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.9","pages":[13,14]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["As a legitimate and innovative financial service","As a form of cheating and fraud, since financial assets should be based on real assets and business activities","As acceptable so long as it is legally documented","As irrelevant to Islamic finance principles"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch1-22","type":"mcq","prompt":"By the mid-2000s, roughly how many countries were practicing Islamic banking and finance, per Ayub?","explanation":"The chapter states Islamic banking and finance was being practiced in over 75 countries around the world, with about 550 Islamic financial institutions.","topicId":"t1.10","chapter":1,"section":"1.10","topicLabel":"Islamic Finance's Growth and Global Milestones","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Describe the milestones Islamic finance had reached globally by the mid-2000s","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.10","pages":[15,16]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Over 25","Over 50","Over 75","Over 150"],"correctAnswer":2},{"id":"q-ch1-23","type":"identify","prompt":"Identify the standard-setting body responsible for accounting and auditing standards for Islamic financial institutions.","explanation":"AAOIFI (Accounting and Auditing Organization for Islamic Financial Institutions) is the body responsible for accounting/auditing standards; IFSB covers financial services standards more broadly, IIFM covers financial market conventions, and LMC addresses liquidity management.","topicId":"t1.10","chapter":1,"section":"1.10","topicLabel":"Islamic Finance's Growth and Global Milestones","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Describe the milestones Islamic finance had reached globally by the mid-2000s","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.10","pages":[15,16]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["IFSB","AAOIFI","IIFM","LMC"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch1-24","type":"mcq","prompt":"What practice does Ayub criticize among some Islamic financial institutions (IFIs)?","explanation":"Ayub criticizes IFIs that look at conventional products and mould them to formally/legally fit the Islamic perspective without regard to their impact on society, arguing this undermines the integrity of Islamic finance theory.","topicId":"t1.11","chapter":1,"section":"1.11","topicLabel":"Could Islamic Finance Achieve Its Objectives? — Outlook and the Book's Roadmap","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Explain the three-part structure of the book and what each part covers","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.11","pages":[16,17]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["Charging too little profit on financing","Reshaping conventional products to formally/legally 'fit' Islamic labels without regard to their real socio-economic impact","Refusing to serve non-Muslim customers","Over-investing in research and development"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch1-25","type":"order","prompt":"Order the book's three parts as Ayub describes them (Part I, Part II, Part III) by their main focus, from foundational to applied.","explanation":"Part I covers fundamentals and prohibitions (Ch.1-4); Part II covers Islamic contract law (Ch.5-7); Part III covers financing modes/products and closes with criticism appraisal and the way forward (Ch.8-18).","topicId":"t1.11","chapter":1,"section":"1.11","topicLabel":"Could Islamic Finance Achieve Its Objectives? — Outlook and the Book's Roadmap","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain the three-part structure of the book and what each part covers","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.11","pages":[17,18,19]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"items":["Fundamentals and prohibitions of Islamic economics/finance","Islamic law of contracts (sale, loans/debts, subcontracts)","Financing modes, products, capital markets, Takaful, criticism and the way forward"]},{"id":"q-ch1-26","type":"short","prompt":"Why does Ayub say Riba (interest) receives the most attention among the prohibitions discussed in Part I?","explanation":"The author explains that because the prohibition of Riba is the least controversial issue, more emphasis in discussion has been given to the connotation of the term Riba and how it encompasses various forms of present-day business and finance.","topicId":"t1.11","chapter":1,"section":"1.11","topicLabel":"Could Islamic Finance Achieve Its Objectives? — Outlook and the Book's Roadmap","difficulty":"hard","cognitiveLevel":"analysis","objective":"Explain the three-part structure of the book and what each part covers","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.11","pages":[17,18]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Because he considers the prohibition of Riba the least controversial of the major prohibitions, so more emphasis is given to explaining how the term Riba encompasses various forms of present-day business and finance."},{"id":"q-ch1-27","type":"mcq","prompt":"What does Ayub's footnote on 'Billanthropy' (large-scale giving by big charitable foundations) suggest?","explanation":"Ayub notes that 'Billanthropy' by the biggest charitable foundations has not been able to make a dent on poverty, reinforcing his broader argument that selective spending cannot correct systemic injustice.","topicId":"t1.4","chapter":1,"section":"1.4","topicLabel":"Growth Without Distributive Justice, and the Limits of State Welfare Spending","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Summarize why growth alone (without distributive justice) fails to reduce poverty","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.4","pages":[8]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["That charity alone has successfully solved global poverty","That even large-scale charitable giving has failed to make a dent on poverty, since it cannot fix imbalances created by the system as a whole","That charitable foundations should be legally banned","That Zakat and 'Billanthropy' are functionally identical"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch1-28","type":"tf","prompt":"True or False: Per Ayub's footnote, China and India followed the IMF/World Bank's 'Washington Consensus' policy advice during the 1990s and thereby became the fastest-growing economies.","explanation":"False. China and India did NOT follow the Washington Consensus and instead devised their own policies; both became among the fastest-growing economies, while countries that followed the Consensus's advice suffered.","topicId":"t1.2","chapter":1,"section":"1.2","topicLabel":"Conventional Debt as a Recipe for Exploitation","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"understanding","objective":"Describe the link the author draws between excessive debt, unchecked money creation and global financial instability","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.2","pages":[6]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":false},{"id":"q-ch1-29","type":"short","prompt":"What debt-relief statistics does Ayub cite regarding the 27 countries that received relief, and what offsetting trend limited its impact?","explanation":"This reflects the debt-relief data Ayub cites to show that headline debt-relief statistics overstate real progress against poverty.","topicId":"t1.2","chapter":1,"section":"1.2","topicLabel":"Conventional Debt as a Recipe for Exploitation","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Describe the link the author draws between excessive debt, unchecked money creation and global financial instability","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.2","pages":[5]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"correctAnswer":"Debt relief was given to 27 countries (mostly Sub-Saharan Africa), reducing their debt stock by two-thirds per a World Bank report; developing countries' external debt fell from about 45% of GNI (1999) to about 40% (2003), but domestic debt rose to offset this, and developed-country trade barriers on poor countries' products persisted."},{"id":"q-ch1-30","type":"scenario","prompt":"A UK official notes that Islam historically spread mainly through peaceful trade rather than conquest, and Ayub cites this to make a broader point about Islamic finance. What point is that?","explanation":"Ayub cites Gordon Brown's remark on trade spreading Islam peacefully to argue Islamic finance's trade-based models of financial intermediation can similarly enhance social cohesion between Muslim and non-Muslim communities.","topicId":"t1.9","chapter":1,"section":"1.9","topicLabel":"Regulating Trade and Business: The Role of Government and Sound Money Creation","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"application","objective":"Outline the main elements the author identifies as needed for Islamic principles to correct conventional economics","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.9","pages":[13]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["That Islamic finance's trade-based models can foster closer interaction between Muslim and non-Muslim communities and enhance social cohesion","That trade should be replaced entirely by government mandates","That peaceful trade has no relevance to modern finance","That Islamic finance should avoid dealing with non-Muslims"],"correctAnswer":0},{"id":"q-ch1-31","type":"identify","prompt":"Identify the historical event Ayub cites as marking the end of the last Muslim world power, replaced by European colonies.","explanation":"Ayub describes intellectual regressiveness continuing until the British and French instigated rebellion against Turkish rule and brought about the downfall of the Ottoman Empire, the last Muslim world power, replaced by European colonies until independence after World War II.","topicId":"t1.7","chapter":1,"section":"1.7","topicLabel":"Economics and Religion: The Elements of an Economic System","difficulty":"medium","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Explain the three-part structure of the book and what each part covers","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.7","pages":[11]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"options":["The Battle of Badr","The fall of the Ottoman Empire","The Mongol invasion of Baghdad","The Crusades"],"correctAnswer":1},{"id":"q-ch1-32","type":"short","prompt":"Define Mal (property) as cited by Ayub (per Nabhani) in Chapter 1.","explanation":"This definition, cited from Nabhani, underlies the chapter's discussion of ownership and the elements of an economic system.","topicId":"t1.7","chapter":1,"section":"1.7","topicLabel":"Economics and Religion: The Elements of an Economic System","difficulty":"easy","cognitiveLevel":"recall","objective":"Outline the main elements the author identifies as needed for Islamic principles to correct conventional economics","source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.7","pages":[10,11]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[],"format":"definition","correctAnswer":"Anything that can be possessed, including money (gold, silver), commodities (clothes, foodstuffs), and immovable property (houses, factories); wealth is Mal together with human effort."}],"flashcards":[{"id":"f1.1","front":"“No risk, no gain” — what does it mean in the author’s framework?","back":"The basic juristic principle of Shari’ah: anyone who wants a return must also be liable for the loss, if any. It removes the moral hazard of risk-free gains.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t1.5","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.5","pages":[8,9]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f1.2","front":"Three elements of an economic system","back":"1. Ownership of property, commodities and wealth; 2. Disposal of ownership; 3. Distribution of wealth among the people.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t1.7","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.7","pages":[10,11]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f1.3","front":"Four sources of problems identified in Section 1.8","back":"Unchecked creation of money; reliance on market forces without ethical limits; emphasis on growth/profit without regard to distribution; negative role of the State and regulators.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.8","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[11,13]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f1.4","front":"Uqood-e-Mu‘awadha vs Uqood Ghair Mu‘awadha","back":"Commutative contracts (sale, leasing — profit valid) vs noncommutative/gratuitous contracts (gifts, guarantees, loans — no return).","category":"Arabic terminology","topicId":"t1.8","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[11,13]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f1.5","front":"How does time have value in Islamic finance (Section 1.9)?","back":"Only through the pricing of goods and their usufructs — not through interest on money.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t1.9","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.9","pages":[13,14]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f1.6","front":"Four standard-setting bodies/facilitators named in Ch. 1","back":"AAOIFI, IFSB, IIFM and the Liquidity Management Centre (LMC).","category":"Banking","topicId":"t1.10","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.10","pages":[15,16]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f1.7","front":"Zakat rates mentioned in Chapter 1","back":"Generally 2.5% of net wealth; 5 or 10% on agricultural produce above a minimum limit.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.8","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[11,13]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f1.8","front":"Formal vs Shari’ah-inspired compliance","back":"The author warns that moulding conventional products to fit Islamic legal forms without regard to impact undermines Islamic finance; compliance must be Shari’ah inspired.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t1.11","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.11","pages":[16,17]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f1.9","front":"Which interpretation does the book follow?","back":"The mainstream view — widely recognised and the basis of AAOIFI and IFSB standards; the minority view is noted in places.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.12","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.12","pages":[17,19]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f1.10","front":"Why does the author favour risk-related capital over borrowed funds?","back":"Borrowed funds are mainly squandered; risk-related (direct and portfolio) investment links returns to real performance and reduces the debt burden.","category":"Risk","topicId":"t1.3","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.3","pages":[6,8]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f1.11","front":"Bretton Woods and 1971","back":"Bretton Woods (1944) linked currencies to the Dollar at $35 per ounce of gold; in 1971 the gold link was abolished and gold became an ordinary commodity.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.1","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.1","pages":[3,4]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f1.12","front":"US Gini coefficient trend cited in Chapter 1","back":"0.394 (1970) → 0.408 (1990) → 0.462 (2000): growth accompanied by rising inequality.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.3","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.3","pages":[6,8]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f1.13","front":"Why is welfare spending not enough (Section 1.4)?","back":"Selective state spending cannot correct system-wide imbalances and may cause moral hazard; weaker groups must get their share at the production and distribution stage.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t1.4","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.4","pages":[8,8]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f1.14","front":"Benefits of prohibiting interest (Section 1.6)","back":"A moral dimension in finance, greater equity and market discipline, and a healthier, more stable system.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t1.6","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.6","pages":[9,10]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f1.15","front":"Malaysia’s New Economic Policy","back":"Adopted after trickle-down growth ended in the 1969 riots; twin aims — eradicate poverty and restructure society with justice — credited for later success.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.7","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.7","pages":[10,11]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f1.16","front":"Two criteria for money creation (Section 1.9)","back":"(1) All financial assets must be based on real assets; (2) time has value only through the pricing of goods and usufructs.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t1.9","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.9","pages":[13,14]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f1.17","front":"Can a guarantor charge a fee?","back":"No — guaranteeing a financial obligation for a price involves Riba; only out-of-pocket expenses may be recovered (Fiqh Academy).","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t1.8","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[11,13]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"f1.18","front":"Scale of Islamic finance cited in Chapter 1","back":"Over 75 countries, about 550 institutions; about 40% of Malaysian Islamic bank clients are non-Muslims.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.10","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.10","pages":[15,16]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-1","front":"According to Ayub, what happened to the gold-Dollar link in 1971, and why does it matter?","back":"The gold-currency link established at Bretton Woods (1944) was abolished in 1971. Gold became a normal commodity priced in currency rather than a unit of account, and the US Dollar (as reserve currency) became the basis for a financial system the author says enabled US deficits and 'oceans' of global poverty.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.1","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.1","pages":[3,4]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-2","front":"What does Ayub quote Keynes as saying about private and social interest?","back":"That the world is not so governed that private and social interests always coincide, and that self-interest generally is not 'enlightened' enough to serve the public interest on its own.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t1.1","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.1","pages":[3,4]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-3","front":"Why does Ayub call conventional debt 'a recipe for exploitation'?","back":"Because interest-based debt tends to be endlessly rolled over rather than repaid, diverting government revenue to debt-servicing, shifting the balance from risk-based to risk-free capital, and producing business failure, unemployment and wealth concentration.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.2","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.2","pages":[4,5,6]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-4","front":"What Gini coefficient trend does Ayub cite for the US, and what does it show?","back":"The US Gini coefficient rose from 0.394 (1970) to 0.408 (1990) to 0.462 (2000), showing rising income inequality even in a developed, growing economy.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.2","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.2","pages":[4,5,6]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-5","front":"In Islamic Shari'ah, how is debt liability treated, per Ayub, compared to conventional debt accountability?","back":"Debt liability in Shari'ah is subject to strict accountability on the Day of Judgement — it persists even in insolvency until the creditor waives it — unlike conventional debt where accountability for eventual repayment is diffuse.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t1.2","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.2","pages":[4,5]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-6","front":"What does Ayub say is wrong with 'trickle-down theory' (TDT)?","back":"TDT and steady-state growth models have been shown to increase asset-distribution inequality, since already-powerful groups grow even faster than before, leaving the masses in deeper misery (he cites China's rural poor despite double-digit growth).","category":"Principles","topicId":"t1.3","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.3","pages":[6,7,8]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-7","front":"Why can't state social-welfare spending alone fix the injustices Ayub describes?","back":"Because it cannot correct imbalances created by the system as a whole, can create moral hazard, and cannot reach the scale of need — the real fix must occur at the production/distribution stage itself, not just via after-the-fact compensatory spending.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t1.3","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.3","pages":[8]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-8","front":"According to Ayub, what are the two main factors ('the main culprit') behind global economic injustice?","back":"(1) An inefficient economic-management model with no real concern for poverty/exploitation, and (2) the functioning of money, finance and financial markets — with the institution of interest singled out as the central culprit.","category":"Prohibitions","topicId":"t1.5","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.5","pages":[8,9]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-9","front":"State the 'no risk, no gain' principle as presented by Ayub.","back":"The basic juristic principle of Shari'ah and a normative rule of justice: there can be no legitimate gain without risk-sharing — whoever wants a return must also bear liability for the possible loss.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t1.5","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.5","pages":[8,9]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-10","front":"Define Riba as given in Chapter 1 (introductory sense).","back":"An increase over the principal in a loan/debt transaction, or in an exchange transaction, accruing to the lender/creditor or a party to the exchange without an equivalent counter-value ('iwad) given in return.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t1.5","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.5","pages":[8,9]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-11","front":"Per Ayub, what does a majority of Muslim jurists/scholars identify as the ultimate cause of financial crises?","back":"Disregarding the prohibition of interest, which Ayub frames as a teaching common to all major (revealed) religions, not unique to Islam.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.6","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.6","pages":[9,10]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-12","front":"How does Ayub distinguish 'economics' as a science from an 'economic system'?","back":"Economics (as a science) deals with the creation of wealth; an economic system is an ideology-based framework for managing the distribution of wealth in society (ownership, disposal of ownership, and distribution among people).","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t1.7","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.7","pages":[10,11]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-13","front":"What are the three elements that make up any economic system, per Ayub?","back":"1) Ownership of property, commodities and wealth; 2) Disposal of ownership; 3) Distribution of wealth among the people.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.7","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.7","pages":[10,11]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-14","front":"What happened in Malaysia in 1957-1970 and how did policy change afterward?","back":"Malaysia's trickle-down-theory policy failed and contributed to the 13th May 1969 race riots. The government then adopted the New Economic Policy, applying justice-with-fairness (though not full Shari'ah), credited with Malaysia's later economic success.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.7","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.7","pages":[10,11]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-15","front":"Distinguish commutative (Uqood-e-Mu'awadha) from noncommutative (Uqood Ghair Mu'awadha) contracts.","back":"Commutative contracts (e.g. sale, leasing) involve consideration on both sides and permit profit. Noncommutative contracts (e.g. gifts, guarantees, loans) are gratuitous/benevolent acts, so no return/profit may be taken.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t1.8","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[11,12,13]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-16","front":"How is Zakat calculated and who receives it, per Chapter 1?","back":"Generally 2.5% of net wealth above the Nisab threshold (5% or 10% for qualifying agricultural produce), payable by every Muslim whose wealth exceeds consumption needs, distributed among the have-nots and needy per Qur'an 9:60.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t1.8","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[11,12]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-17","front":"Can a guarantor (Kafalah) charge a fee for providing a guarantee, per Ayub's footnote?","back":"No — charging for a guarantee involves Riba and is prohibited; the guarantor may only recover actual out-of-pocket expenses incurred, not an opportunity-cost fee.","category":"Contract rules","topicId":"t1.8","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.8","pages":[12,13]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-18","front":"What two priorities does Ayub propose for economists/policymakers to reform the global economic system (Section 1.9)?","back":"(1) An active government oversight role (not business-running) ensuring accountable, transparent market functioning; (2) linking money/finance/financial instruments to genuine, real assets and business transactions rather than creating money 'out of nothing'.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t1.9","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.9","pages":[13,14]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-19","front":"How does Islamic finance realize 'time value of money', per Ayub, instead of charging interest?","back":"Only through the pricing of goods and their usufructs (benefits/use) in genuine trading/leasing transactions — not as an independent charge (interest) on money itself.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t1.9","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.9","pages":[13,14]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-20","front":"By the mid-2000s, roughly how many countries and institutions practiced Islamic banking/finance, per Ayub?","back":"Over 75 countries, with about 550 Islamic financial institutions.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.10","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.10","pages":[15,16]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-21","front":"Name the four key Islamic finance standard-setting/infrastructure bodies mentioned in Chapter 1.","back":"AAOIFI (Accounting and Auditing Organization for Islamic Financial Institutions), IFSB (Islamic Financial Services Board), IIFM (International Islamic Financial Market), and LMC (Liquidity Management Centre).","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.10","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.10","pages":[15,16]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-22","front":"What does Ayub warn against regarding how some IFIs implement 'Shari'ah compliance'?","back":"He warns against IFIs that merely reshape conventional products to formally/legally 'fit' Islamic labels without regard for real socio-economic impact — arguing compliance should be substantively 'Shari'ah inspired', not just formally compliant.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t1.11","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.11","pages":[16,17]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-23","front":"What does Part I of Ayub's book cover, and which prohibition gets the most attention?","back":"Part I (Ch.1-4) covers the need for, basis and structure of Islamic economics and the main prohibitions IFIs must observe. Riba (interest) receives the most attention, as the author calls it the least controversial prohibition.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.11","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.11","pages":[17,18]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-24","front":"What do Parts II and III of Ayub's book cover?","back":"Part II (Ch.5-7): Islamic law of contracts — sale, loans/debts, subcontracts like Hawalah/Kafalah/Rahn. Part III (Ch.8-18): financing modes and products (Murabaha, Salam/Istisna'a, Ijarah, Musharakah/Mudarabah), accessory contracts, application principles, Sukuk, Takaful, an appraisal of criticism, and the way forward.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.11","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.11","pages":[17,18,19]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-25","front":"What does Ayub say about 'Billanthropy' by the biggest charitable foundations?","back":"Even large-scale giving ('Billanthropy') by the biggest charitable foundations has not been able to make a dent on poverty, since imbalances created by the system as a whole cannot be corrected by selective spending alone.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.4","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.4","pages":[8]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-26","front":"What UN estimate does John Perkins cite (via Ayub) to contrast with global war spending?","back":"The United Nations estimated that about $40 billion would be sufficient to provide clean water, adequate diets, sanitation and basic education to every person on the planet - a sum Perkins contrasts with the trillions spent on wars and bribing the corrupt.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.2","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.2","pages":[5,6]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-27","front":"Per Ayub's footnote, how did China and India's response to the 'Washington Consensus' differ from countries that followed it, and what happened in Malaysia's 1997-98 crisis?","back":"China and India did not follow the Washington Consensus (jointly devised by the US Treasury, IMF and World Bank) and instead devised their own policies, becoming the fastest-growing economies in the world; Malaysia controlled its late-1990s crisis by adopting its own policies rather than IMF/World Bank advice.","category":"Comparisons","topicId":"t1.2","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.2","pages":[6]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-28","front":"What debt-relief statistics does Ayub cite for developing countries in the early-to-mid 2000s?","back":"Debt relief was given to 27 countries (mostly Sub-Saharan Africa), reducing their debt stock by two-thirds (World Bank, 2005); developing countries' external debt fell from about 45% of GNI (1999) to about 40% (2003) - though domestic debt rose to offset this, and developed-country trade barriers on poor countries' products persisted.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.2","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.2","pages":[5]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-29","front":"Define Mal (property) per Nabhani, as cited by Ayub in Chapter 1.","back":"Anything that can be possessed: money (e.g. gold and silver), commodities (e.g. clothes and foodstuffs), and immovable property (e.g. houses and factories); wealth is Mal together with human effort.","category":"Definitions","topicId":"t1.7","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.7","pages":[10,11]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-30","front":"What did UK Chancellor Gordon Brown say about how Islam historically spread, and how does Ayub connect this to Islamic finance?","back":"Brown said it was mainly through peaceful trade that the faith of Islam arrived in different countries. Ayub argues Islamic finance's trade-based models can similarly provide closer interaction between Muslim and non-Muslim communities and enhance social cohesion.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.9","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.9","pages":[13]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-31","front":"Summarize the historical arc of Muslim civilization's economic role that Ayub sketches in Section 1.7.","back":"A golden age of trade, scholarship and economic-principle development from the 7th to 11th century, followed by intellectual decline that caused the Muslim world to miss the Industrial Revolution, ending with the fall of the Ottoman Empire (the last Muslim world power) to European colonization after British/French-instigated rebellion against Turkish rule.","category":"Exam facts","topicId":"t1.7","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.7","pages":[11]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"fc-ch1-32","front":"What Hadith does Gordon Brown quote (cited by Ayub) to argue for cross-border ethical solidarity?","back":"'The Ummah, the Muslim global community, is like the human body - when one part feels pain, the other parts must reflect the pain' - a truth relevant in and beyond the Muslim world, emphasizing duty to strangers and outsiders.","category":"Principles","topicId":"t1.9","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.9","pages":[14]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}],"exam":[{"id":"e1.1","kind":"long","prompt":"“Conventional debt is a recipe for exploitation.” Discuss this statement in the light of Chapter 1 and explain the alternative the author proposes.","expectedStructure":["Introduce the author’s critique of the neoclassical/post-Bretton Woods order","Explain how interest-based debt becomes self-perpetuating and who bears the cost","Show effects: risk-free vs risk-based capital, inequality, instability","Note that debt relief and welfare spending have not solved the problem","Present the alternative: risk-sharing, asset-based finance, trading/leasing/partnership","Conclude with the need for Shari’ah-inspired implementation"],"keyConcepts":["Riba/interest","risk-sharing","no risk, no gain","asset-backing","distributive justice"],"essentialPoints":["Yesterday’s debt repaid with today’s debt","Debt servicing through taxes and export earnings","Share of risk-free capital rises","Replace with risk-related capital and direct/portfolio investment","Financial assets must be based on real assets"],"commonMistakes":["Presenting the argument as rejecting markets altogether","Omitting the author’s proposed alternative","Quoting statistics as current rather than as the author’s figures"],"topicId":"t1.2","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.2","pages":[4,6]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e1.2","kind":"short","prompt":"Distinguish between economics as a science and an economic system. What are the three elements of an economic system?","expectedStructure":["Define economic science","Define economic system","List the three elements","State how the Islamic system differs"],"keyConcepts":["wealth creation","ideology","ownership","distribution"],"essentialPoints":["Science: creation of wealth","System: ideology-based management of wealth distribution","Ownership; disposal of ownership; distribution","Islamic system differs in ownership and distribution with a State role against injustice"],"commonMistakes":["Treating the two as the same","Missing ‘disposal of ownership’"],"topicId":"t1.7","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.7","pages":[10,11]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e1.3","kind":"conceptual","prompt":"Explain the principle “no risk, no gain” and its significance for the financial system.","expectedStructure":["State the principle","Link to prohibition of interest","Explain effects on investor behaviour and moral hazard","Relate to later modes (trading, leasing, partnership)"],"keyConcepts":["risk-sharing","liability for loss","moral hazard","discipline"],"essentialPoints":["Return requires liability for loss","Careful investment","Removes moral hazard of risk-free gains","Injects discipline"],"commonMistakes":["Claiming risk must be maximised rather than borne"],"topicId":"t1.5","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.5","pages":[8,9]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e1.4","kind":"viva","prompt":"What is the difference between formal Shari’ah compliance and Shari’ah-inspired compliance?","expectedStructure":["One-line definition of each","Author’s warning","Implication for product development"],"keyConcepts":["form vs substance","integrity"],"essentialPoints":["Formal: fits legal form","Inspired: considers basis and social impact","Mimicking conventional products undermines integrity"],"commonMistakes":["Suggesting formal compliance is invalid rather than insufficient"],"topicId":"t1.11","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.11","pages":[16,17]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"e1.5","kind":"long","prompt":"Critically assess the author’s claim that growth alone cannot deliver socio-economic justice. What alternative framework does he propose?","expectedStructure":["Growth versus development","Evidence: US Gini trend, China, trickle-down failure","Why welfare spending is insufficient","Main culprits: economic management and money/finance","Proposed framework: risk-related capital, real-asset basis, active state oversight","Conclusion"],"keyConcepts":["trickle-down","Gini coefficient","risk-related capital","no risk, no gain","real assets"],"essentialPoints":["Growth can widen asset inequality","Redistribution needed before stabilisation","Replace borrowed funds with direct/portfolio investment","State as overseer, not business operator"],"commonMistakes":["Claiming Islam rejects growth or markets","Ignoring the state’s role","No evidence from the chapter"],"topicId":"t1.3","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.3","pages":[6,8]},"origin":["f1"],"aliases":[]},{"id":"et-19","kind":"answer-plan","prompt":"Explain the book's core critique of the conventional interest-based financial system and why Islamic finance is proposed as an alternative.","expectedStructure":["State how the interest-based system creates exploitation and unrepayable debt","Explain the 'no risk, no gain' principle as the Shari'ah alternative's rationale","Note the case for replacing risk-free capital with risk-related capital","Conclude with what Islamic finance principles are meant to correct"],"keyConcepts":["Interest-based system","Risk-related capital","No risk, no gain","Distributive justice"],"essentialPoints":["The interest-based system is described as creating unrepayable debt, making one class of people richer while leaving others poorer and oppressed, since yesterday's debt is often repaid only by taking on more debt today.","The institution of interest, by which governments and corporations borrow, 'creates parasites in society' and widens the gap between rich and poor, echoed in Yusuf Ali's translation of verse 2:275 describing usury as breeding 'idlers' and 'cruel bloodsuckers'.","The prohibition of interest across revealed religions implies 'no gain without risk-sharing' — a party seeking a return must also bear liability for loss — which the book calls the basic juristic principle of Shari'ah and a normative rule of justice.","The proposed solution is to replace risk-free, interest-based capital allocation with risk-related placement under partnership, trading and leasing contracts, so that fund owners share both the risk and the return with the users of funds."],"commonMistakes":["Reducing the critique to 'interest is simply forbidden by religion' without capturing the book's economic argument about excessive debt, exploitation and distributive injustice.","Missing that the proposed alternative is not merely 'no interest' but a structural shift to risk-and-liability-based entitlement to profit."],"topicId":"t1.5","chapter":1,"source":{"chapter":1,"section":"1.5","pages":[8,9]},"origin":["f2"],"aliases":[]}]});
